1
00:00:01,021 --> 00:00:06,027
TOBACCO USE IS DEPICTED

2
00:00:50,822 --> 00:00:51,906
Stand up!

3
00:00:56,452 --> 00:01:00,331
INDIANA JONES AND THE DIAL OF DESTINY

4
00:01:13,011 --> 00:01:14,220
An American, Colonel.

5
00:01:14,512 --> 00:01:16,514
He was at the gate,
posing as an officer.

6
00:01:23,062 --> 00:01:24,189
Was he alone?

7
00:01:25,815 --> 00:01:28,818
The spy, was he alone?

8
00:01:28,902 --> 00:01:30,320
I like being alone.

9
00:01:31,696 --> 00:01:33,406
What did you come here for?

10
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,784
You have lots of pretty things.

11
00:01:36,868 --> 00:01:38,203
Things that belong to others.

12
00:01:39,913 --> 00:01:41,998
"To the victor go the spoils."

13
00:01:43,249 --> 00:01:44,417
"The victor"?

14
00:01:45,418 --> 00:01:47,003
Berlin is in ruins.

15
00:01:47,086 --> 00:01:48,713
The Führer is hiding.

16
00:01:49,923 --> 00:01:51,674
You lost.

17
00:01:55,553 --> 00:01:57,013
Take him upstairs.

18
00:01:57,597 --> 00:01:58,514
What?

19
00:01:58,598 --> 00:01:59,641
Hey!

20
00:01:59,724 --> 00:02:00,892
Wait. Boys!

21
00:02:00,975 --> 00:02:02,268
One moment. Please!

22
00:02:03,061 --> 00:02:04,562
Stop! I need to speak
to the commanding officer.

23
00:02:04,854 --> 00:02:06,731
Colonel. I found it!

24
00:02:08,815 --> 00:02:10,193
Do as he says. Open it.

25
00:02:24,582 --> 00:02:26,584
The Lance of Longinus.

26
00:02:28,127 --> 00:02:30,421
The spear that spilled Christ's blood.

27
00:02:31,297 --> 00:02:32,549
The Holy Lance.

28
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:45,103
Double the guard.
This is the prize the Führer wants.

29
00:02:45,186 --> 00:02:46,145
Colonel, we need to talk.

30
00:02:46,229 --> 00:02:47,397
There's no time, Doctor.

31
00:02:47,647 --> 00:02:49,023
The train to Berlin is waiting for us.

32
00:02:50,525 --> 00:02:51,359
Search the woods!

33
00:02:51,651 --> 00:02:52,485
Bring the dogs.

34
00:02:53,528 --> 00:02:54,904
Do you think that spy was alone?

35
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:57,031
Now! Now! Now! Move out!

36
00:02:57,323 --> 00:02:58,157
Quickly!

37
00:03:24,142 --> 00:03:26,519
Looks like they're leaving without you.

38
00:03:27,812 --> 00:03:29,939
Tell us your story or die.

39
00:03:30,732 --> 00:03:31,566
A story.

40
00:03:32,942 --> 00:03:34,861
Well.

41
00:03:34,944 --> 00:03:35,904
All right...

42
00:03:36,779 --> 00:03:38,281
It all began...

43
00:03:38,948 --> 00:03:43,119
...once upon a time, when this village...

44
00:03:43,203 --> 00:03:47,707
...of stupid blue-eyed boys
decided to join hands...

45
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:51,085
...and follow a sweaty Pied Piper
named Adolf...

46
00:04:11,356 --> 00:04:12,232
No.

47
00:05:30,894 --> 00:05:32,979
This man was with the American.

48
00:05:34,647 --> 00:05:35,732
It's the American's bag.

49
00:05:37,609 --> 00:05:38,568
What did I do?

50
00:05:43,156 --> 00:05:44,490
Take him to my carriage.

51
00:05:52,373 --> 00:05:55,168
Be careful.
It's the Führer's special relic.

52
00:06:24,739 --> 00:06:26,032
Stay seated.

53
00:06:26,908 --> 00:06:28,243
So...

54
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,039
Are you a birdwatcher?

55
00:06:36,709 --> 00:06:38,586
Yes. Because of the bombing...

56
00:06:38,878 --> 00:06:41,506
...the wagtails have been taking
a completely different route.

57
00:06:45,468 --> 00:06:47,178
We've caught your accomplice.

58
00:06:48,388 --> 00:06:49,889
The American.

59
00:07:55,538 --> 00:07:56,456
Good heavens!

60
00:08:22,690 --> 00:08:23,900
Let me go!

61
00:08:52,262 --> 00:08:54,847
You're still alive for a reason,
birdwatcher.

62
00:08:57,517 --> 00:08:59,143
Who sent you?

63
00:08:59,227 --> 00:09:00,478
What was your mission?

64
00:09:00,562 --> 00:09:02,647
No, Colonel. Please.

65
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:05,024
My name is Basil Shaw.

66
00:09:06,276 --> 00:09:08,027
I'm a professor at Oxford.

67
00:09:08,111 --> 00:09:09,737
I'm an archaeologist.

68
00:09:17,328 --> 00:09:18,413
There you are.

69
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:24,586
There's a problem.

70
00:09:25,628 --> 00:09:26,921
I need to speak to the Colonel.

71
00:09:27,422 --> 00:09:29,215
It's urgent.

72
00:09:31,593 --> 00:09:33,511
I have a daughter. I beg you.

73
00:09:34,137 --> 00:09:36,848
I guarantee you
you'll never see your daughter again...

74
00:09:40,184 --> 00:09:43,146
...unless you explain
why your accomplice had this.

75
00:10:05,710 --> 00:10:09,839
We were told the Lance of Longinus
might be at the fortress.

76
00:10:10,757 --> 00:10:11,633
We were looking for it.

77
00:10:12,675 --> 00:10:13,676
Why?

78
00:10:15,261 --> 00:10:16,304
For its power?

79
00:10:17,430 --> 00:10:20,141
It has no power.

80
00:10:22,769 --> 00:10:25,396
My friend and I were trying
to preserve history.

81
00:10:28,024 --> 00:10:29,484
I need to speak to the Colonel.

82
00:10:30,777 --> 00:10:31,819
It's about the lance.

83
00:11:18,575 --> 00:11:19,951
I only saw it for a moment.

84
00:11:20,159 --> 00:11:21,870
My specialty is physics.

85
00:11:22,370 --> 00:11:24,330
For God's sake, spit it out!

86
00:11:26,416 --> 00:11:27,250
The lance...

87
00:11:29,836 --> 00:11:31,546
It's a fake.

88
00:11:32,171 --> 00:11:33,006
It's a fake.

89
00:11:33,423 --> 00:11:34,549
A fake?

90
00:11:36,801 --> 00:11:38,428
The lance is made from an alloy.

91
00:11:38,511 --> 00:11:39,846
It's fifty years old.

92
00:11:40,054 --> 00:11:42,181
The engraving is recent. It's a replica.

93
00:11:42,390 --> 00:11:43,308
We're dead.

94
00:11:46,185 --> 00:11:48,438
Twelfth century. Thirteenth century.

95
00:11:49,189 --> 00:11:50,732
Ramses II.

96
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:53,860
None of these are fakes.

97
00:11:56,696 --> 00:11:58,281
I have to stop this train.

98
00:11:58,615 --> 00:11:59,949
There's another relic on this train.

99
00:12:01,117 --> 00:12:02,535
One with real power.

100
00:12:02,994 --> 00:12:04,120
What are you talking about?

101
00:12:06,164 --> 00:12:07,081
The Antikythera.

102
00:12:07,790 --> 00:12:08,625
The Antikythera?

103
00:12:08,708 --> 00:12:10,710
Enough about that old dial!

104
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:13,129
Colonel, the Führer has lost the war...

105
00:12:13,546 --> 00:12:14,923
...and his mind.

106
00:12:17,258 --> 00:12:18,468
Let me explain!

107
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:22,931
The power of the Antikythera
isn't supernatural.

108
00:12:23,389 --> 00:12:25,266
It's mathematics.

109
00:12:26,976 --> 00:12:28,478
Whoever possesses it...

110
00:12:30,563 --> 00:12:32,065
...won't be a king...

111
00:12:32,815 --> 00:12:33,816
...or an emperor...

112
00:12:35,193 --> 00:12:36,402
...or a Führer.

113
00:12:39,906 --> 00:12:41,699
He'll be God.

114
00:13:00,218 --> 00:13:01,761
There are too many Nazis.

115
00:13:05,598 --> 00:13:08,226
And how do you propose we tell him?

116
00:13:08,893 --> 00:13:09,727
Führer,

117
00:13:10,270 --> 00:13:12,313
I'm sorry the spear of Christ is a fake.

118
00:13:12,564 --> 00:13:13,731
But we bring you...

119
00:13:15,149 --> 00:13:17,902
...half of something you don't even know about.

120
00:13:19,445 --> 00:13:20,405
Tell me.

121
00:13:20,864 --> 00:13:22,365
Do you know Hitler?

122
00:13:24,951 --> 00:13:26,035
There's a saboteur on board.

123
00:13:26,244 --> 00:13:27,537
And Hitler's lance has disappeared.

124
00:14:08,912 --> 00:14:10,121
Over there!

125
00:14:13,791 --> 00:14:15,084
What are you doing here?

126
00:14:22,383 --> 00:14:23,218
What the hell?

127
00:14:24,719 --> 00:14:26,179
Indy?

128
00:14:26,262 --> 00:14:27,096
Baz?

129
00:14:27,597 --> 00:14:28,431
You're alive!

130
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:30,141
For now.

131
00:14:31,017 --> 00:14:33,311
I told you to stay in the woods.

132
00:14:33,394 --> 00:14:37,482
What kind of man hides
while his friend faces death?

133
00:14:50,662 --> 00:14:54,499
This group is taking
half the world's antiquities.

134
00:14:54,582 --> 00:14:57,627
I was going to stop them,
but now I have to rescue you.

135
00:14:58,253 --> 00:14:59,712
Did you at least find the lance?

136
00:15:00,129 --> 00:15:01,965
- "At least"?
- Have you got it?

137
00:15:02,465 --> 00:15:03,383
It's a fake.

138
00:15:03,466 --> 00:15:04,300
What?

139
00:15:05,009 --> 00:15:06,302
A replica.

140
00:15:09,138 --> 00:15:09,973
Who are you?

141
00:15:14,519 --> 00:15:15,395
Indy?

142
00:15:24,445 --> 00:15:25,738
The Antikythera.

143
00:15:26,906 --> 00:15:28,575
Archimedes' dial.

144
00:15:30,118 --> 00:15:31,119
Get it.

145
00:15:48,845 --> 00:15:49,971
Hurry up, Baz.

146
00:15:52,891 --> 00:15:54,350
Give me that.

147
00:15:54,434 --> 00:15:55,560
Come on.

148
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:11,868
Get up, Baz.

149
00:16:11,951 --> 00:16:13,161
Follow me.

150
00:16:13,828 --> 00:16:15,205
Toward the gun?

151
00:16:15,288 --> 00:16:16,581
Away from the Nazis!

152
00:16:16,998 --> 00:16:18,958
Those are Nazis!

153
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,502
Get down!

154
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:11,927
This way. Come on!

155
00:17:12,595 --> 00:17:13,596
I can't do it!

156
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:15,598
Do you want to stop for a rest?

157
00:17:19,394 --> 00:17:20,770
They've got the Antikythera!

158
00:17:22,187 --> 00:17:23,188
Calm down.

159
00:17:54,262 --> 00:17:55,096
A tunnel!

160
00:18:05,899 --> 00:18:07,442
I've got you! No!

161
00:18:47,857 --> 00:18:49,108
Indy!

162
00:18:57,283 --> 00:18:58,368
Grab the gun!

163
00:19:07,544 --> 00:19:08,962
Shoot him!

164
00:19:11,881 --> 00:19:12,966
Not me!

165
00:19:14,092 --> 00:19:15,093
Sorry.

166
00:19:34,404 --> 00:19:36,406
To the victor go the spoils.

167
00:20:07,395 --> 00:20:09,105
Drop the gun.

168
00:20:11,858 --> 00:20:13,401
Give me the Antikythera.

169
00:20:24,454 --> 00:20:25,872
Indy!

170
00:20:36,841 --> 00:20:37,675
Hey!

171
00:20:40,762 --> 00:20:43,097
- Here!
- They can't hear you, Baz.

172
00:20:43,640 --> 00:20:45,099
We have to jump!

173
00:20:45,183 --> 00:20:46,517
What about my bad knee?

174
00:20:53,483 --> 00:20:54,484
Baz!

175
00:20:55,401 --> 00:20:56,402
Baz!

176
00:20:58,238 --> 00:20:59,280
Baz!

177
00:21:00,615 --> 00:21:02,283
- Indy!
- Baz!

178
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:09,082
Come on! Hurry!

179
00:21:10,833 --> 00:21:13,711
It's frustrating
to come back empty-handed after all that.

180
00:21:14,295 --> 00:21:15,588
"Empty-handed"?

181
00:21:15,672 --> 00:21:17,048
Not exactly.

182
00:21:19,801 --> 00:21:21,010
Archimedes' dial.

183
00:21:21,094 --> 00:21:22,220
Half of it.

184
00:21:22,303 --> 00:21:24,514
Come on, Baz. Let's go home.

185
00:22:29,871 --> 00:22:32,498
Larry! Turn it down!

186
00:22:33,541 --> 00:22:34,876
Turn it down!

187
00:22:34,959 --> 00:22:36,794
Hey, Larry!

188
00:22:51,517 --> 00:22:52,518
Larry!

189
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:54,979
Larry!

190
00:22:55,063 --> 00:22:56,648
- Hello, Mr. Jones.
- Where's Larry?

191
00:22:57,982 --> 00:22:58,816
Who's that?

192
00:22:58,900 --> 00:23:00,068
The old man next door.

193
00:23:00,151 --> 00:23:01,861
Larry, it's eight in the morning.

194
00:23:01,945 --> 00:23:04,405
- "It's eight in the morning."
- We've talked about this.

195
00:23:04,489 --> 00:23:05,532
Yes, but that was...

196
00:23:05,615 --> 00:23:08,076
- ...a workday, Mr. Jones.
- Today is a workday too!

197
00:23:09,452 --> 00:23:10,703
Put the news on.

198
00:23:10,787 --> 00:23:12,163
It's Moon Day.

199
00:23:13,289 --> 00:23:14,499
Moon Day?

200
00:23:41,943 --> 00:23:44,070
STATE OF NEW YORK
SEPARATION AGREEMENT BETWEEN:

201
00:23:44,153 --> 00:23:47,866
MARION RAVENWOOD AND DR.
HENRY WALTON JONES JR.

202
00:24:07,886 --> 00:24:08,720
Thank you.

203
00:24:22,317 --> 00:24:24,235
All you need to remember...

204
00:24:24,319 --> 00:24:27,989
...is that Assyrian pottery from this period
is characterized...

205
00:24:28,072 --> 00:24:32,118
...by this complex pattern of blue lines.

206
00:24:32,202 --> 00:24:33,203
All right?

207
00:24:34,287 --> 00:24:37,749
I assigned you to read
pages 131 to 171...

208
00:24:37,832 --> 00:24:39,542
...of Winford for today.

209
00:24:39,626 --> 00:24:41,127
Did anyone read it?

210
00:24:43,588 --> 00:24:44,422
Nobody?

211
00:24:47,217 --> 00:24:49,344
Good grief, it's on the exam.

212
00:24:49,427 --> 00:24:50,637
All right.

213
00:24:51,846 --> 00:24:53,848
I suppose I'll have to spoon-feed it to you.

214
00:24:56,726 --> 00:24:59,437
In 213 BC...

215
00:24:59,520 --> 00:25:01,606
...the Roman forces of Marcellus...

216
00:25:01,689 --> 00:25:04,609
...laid siege to the city of Syracuse.

217
00:25:04,692 --> 00:25:06,361
"Syracuse."

218
00:25:07,820 --> 00:25:10,490
Not Syracuse, New York, Tonya.

219
00:25:10,573 --> 00:25:12,158
Syracuse in Sicily.

220
00:25:12,951 --> 00:25:15,411
Among the city's defenders...

221
00:25:15,495 --> 00:25:18,581
...was its most famous resident, who was...

222
00:25:20,792 --> 00:25:22,502
Come on, it's on the final.

223
00:25:23,211 --> 00:25:24,796
Archimedes.

224
00:25:25,672 --> 00:25:26,798
Archimedes.

225
00:25:26,881 --> 00:25:28,299
Archimedes, who was a...

226
00:25:28,383 --> 00:25:29,592
Mathematician.

227
00:25:29,676 --> 00:25:30,927
Mathematician.

228
00:25:31,010 --> 00:25:33,680
But more than that, an inventor...

229
00:25:33,763 --> 00:25:36,349
...a brilliant engineer...

230
00:25:36,432 --> 00:25:40,853
...who devised a way to harness
the Mediterranean sun's energy...

231
00:25:40,937 --> 00:25:43,731
...using concave mirrors and focus it...

232
00:25:43,815 --> 00:25:46,568
...on Roman warships
to set them on fire.

233
00:25:46,651 --> 00:25:50,196
He also invented giant iron claws...

234
00:25:50,280 --> 00:25:52,866
...that could lift enemies out of the sea.

235
00:25:55,451 --> 00:25:58,329
But how do we know
that really happened?

236
00:25:59,455 --> 00:26:03,543
What indisputable physical
and archaeological evidence...

237
00:26:03,626 --> 00:26:06,963
- ...do we have for these inventions?
- The Antikythera.

238
00:26:08,339 --> 00:26:10,592
- The Antikythera.
- For a start.

239
00:26:10,675 --> 00:26:12,677
Here they are! In the center!

240
00:26:14,637 --> 00:26:16,055
The astronauts.

241
00:26:16,139 --> 00:26:18,558
This morning's grand parade
will be watched by...

242
00:26:18,641 --> 00:26:21,769
...two and a half million people
lining the sidewalks...

243
00:26:24,814 --> 00:26:26,316
Here it comes. Do you see it?

244
00:26:26,399 --> 00:26:27,817
Here it comes.

245
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:29,777
Hide the cake.

246
00:26:31,487 --> 00:26:33,573
- Surprise!
- Surprise!

247
00:26:37,702 --> 00:26:38,953
For more than a decade...

248
00:26:39,037 --> 00:26:43,541
...our colleague Dr. Jones
has been a loyal servant here at Hunter.

249
00:26:44,959 --> 00:26:46,544
A token of our gratitude.

250
00:26:53,885 --> 00:26:54,802
Wow.

251
00:26:57,889 --> 00:26:59,140
HAPPY RETIREMENT

252
00:26:59,224 --> 00:27:01,142
Thank you for putting up with me.

253
00:27:15,532 --> 00:27:16,366
Here you go.

254
00:27:25,083 --> 00:27:28,211
Astronauts Neil Armstrong,
Mike Collins and Buzz Aldrin...

255
00:27:28,294 --> 00:27:30,338
...can't help
being the center of attention.

256
00:27:30,421 --> 00:27:34,217
At 11:00 a.m. Eastern time,
a grateful nation will honor them...

257
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:37,011
...with a triumphant parade
through New York and Chicago...

258
00:27:37,095 --> 00:27:38,680
...and a dinner in Los Angeles...

259
00:27:38,763 --> 00:27:40,098
What would the ancients say?

260
00:27:40,557 --> 00:27:42,433
Neil, Buzz and Mike...

261
00:27:42,517 --> 00:27:45,353
If they knew we'd walked on the moon.

262
00:27:45,436 --> 00:27:47,063
Speaking as an ancient...

263
00:27:47,146 --> 00:27:49,691
...going to the moon is like going to Reno.

264
00:27:49,774 --> 00:27:51,067
It's in the middle of nowhere...

265
00:27:52,277 --> 00:27:53,736
...and there's no blackjack.

266
00:27:54,779 --> 00:27:56,406
You don't recognize me, do you?

267
00:27:58,741 --> 00:28:00,785
Whatever I've done, I apologize.

268
00:28:01,744 --> 00:28:02,787
I'm Helena.

269
00:28:04,372 --> 00:28:05,415
Helena Shaw.

270
00:28:07,292 --> 00:28:08,543
Wombat?

271
00:28:08,626 --> 00:28:10,295
I haven't heard that in ages.

272
00:28:10,962 --> 00:28:12,630
- You've grown taller.
- Yes.

273
00:28:12,714 --> 00:28:14,007
It's true.

274
00:28:16,134 --> 00:28:17,218
I'm celebrating.

275
00:28:18,344 --> 00:28:20,555
- I'm retiring.
- Oh.

276
00:28:21,055 --> 00:28:22,557
Wow. In that case, what are we drinking?

277
00:28:27,979 --> 00:28:29,731
Room service for Mr. Schmidt.

278
00:28:42,243 --> 00:28:43,286
Put it down.

279
00:28:43,369 --> 00:28:45,205
I asked how you broke your ankle.

280
00:28:45,997 --> 00:28:46,998
None of your business.

281
00:28:47,081 --> 00:28:48,082
Is this Mr. Schmidt?

282
00:28:48,166 --> 00:28:50,376
"Doctor" Schmidt. He's in there.

283
00:28:50,460 --> 00:28:53,254
And he won't eat anything on wheels.
Put it on a table.

284
00:28:54,714 --> 00:28:56,007
When I was a child...

285
00:28:56,507 --> 00:28:57,759
...I always imagined...

286
00:28:57,842 --> 00:29:01,554
...that one day
man would walk on the moon.

287
00:29:02,096 --> 00:29:03,223
And gosh...

288
00:29:09,854 --> 00:29:11,564
...what a celebration out there.

289
00:29:14,984 --> 00:29:16,528
The man you serve...

290
00:29:16,611 --> 00:29:18,988
...put those astronauts on the moon.

291
00:29:19,072 --> 00:29:20,114
He built the rockets.

292
00:29:21,533 --> 00:29:22,784
Congratulations.

293
00:29:23,243 --> 00:29:24,077
Where are you from?

294
00:29:25,161 --> 00:29:26,037
The Bronx.

295
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:28,164
No. Originally, your people.

296
00:29:28,248 --> 00:29:29,249
Do you know?

297
00:29:33,878 --> 00:29:35,588
I was born near Yankee Stadium.

298
00:29:38,174 --> 00:29:40,009
And did you fight for your country?

299
00:29:40,969 --> 00:29:42,470
In the 320th Battalion.

300
00:29:42,554 --> 00:29:44,931
I put up balloons to stop the planes
bombing Normandy.

301
00:29:50,436 --> 00:29:52,272
And are you enjoying your victory?

302
00:30:00,154 --> 00:30:01,155
Anything else?

303
00:30:03,700 --> 00:30:04,909
You didn't win the war.

304
00:30:06,578 --> 00:30:07,704
Hitler lost it.

305
00:30:13,334 --> 00:30:14,168
Yes.

306
00:30:15,461 --> 00:30:18,131
My field agent found Shaw.

307
00:30:19,132 --> 00:30:20,425
Yes, I'm on my way.

308
00:30:20,508 --> 00:30:21,509
Come on, Hauke.

309
00:30:24,846 --> 00:30:26,097
Where are we?

310
00:30:27,181 --> 00:30:29,100
Oxford. In the garden.

311
00:30:30,977 --> 00:30:32,270
He was one of a kind.

312
00:30:34,689 --> 00:30:36,191
I've just graduated.

313
00:30:36,274 --> 00:30:37,275
In archaeology.

314
00:30:38,234 --> 00:30:40,445
Archaeology. Wow.

315
00:30:40,528 --> 00:30:42,155
Like father, like daughter.

316
00:30:42,238 --> 00:30:44,949
Now I'm working on a doctorate.

317
00:30:46,159 --> 00:30:47,160
What's your subject?

318
00:30:47,952 --> 00:30:49,954
Archimedes' dial.

319
00:30:50,622 --> 00:30:52,040
The Antikythera.

320
00:30:55,001 --> 00:30:56,336
What do you know about it?

321
00:30:56,878 --> 00:30:58,296
For starters...

322
00:30:58,379 --> 00:31:01,841
...in 1902, sponge divers found
a huge Roman shipwreck...

323
00:31:01,925 --> 00:31:03,301
...off the coast of Greece.

324
00:31:03,927 --> 00:31:05,720
Below deck, sealed with wax...

325
00:31:05,803 --> 00:31:07,430
...was a clockwork mechanism...

326
00:31:07,513 --> 00:31:10,391
...finely crafted,
of unknown purpose.

327
00:31:10,475 --> 00:31:14,437
Nothing of comparable complexity
appeared for another thousand years.

328
00:31:15,605 --> 00:31:17,815
- You did your homework.
- I didn't.

329
00:31:17,899 --> 00:31:20,985
Dad did. He had newspapers
and reams of notes about it.

330
00:31:21,069 --> 00:31:22,904
It was his obsession until he died.

331
00:31:26,574 --> 00:31:28,743
He said they found it
on a Nazi looting train.

332
00:31:30,203 --> 00:31:33,289
And lost it
in a river in the French Alps.

333
00:31:36,084 --> 00:31:37,335
That was long ago.

334
00:31:37,418 --> 00:31:38,962
And it was half of the dial.

335
00:31:39,045 --> 00:31:40,672
- Archimedes...
- ...split it in two...

336
00:31:40,755 --> 00:31:41,798
- ...dismantled it.
- ...and hid...

337
00:31:41,881 --> 00:31:43,967
...the halves during the siege of Syracuse.

338
00:31:44,050 --> 00:31:45,260
I know. Look.

339
00:31:46,427 --> 00:31:48,263
Don't you remember the last time you saw me?

340
00:31:49,889 --> 00:31:51,099
Remember what?

341
00:31:51,182 --> 00:31:52,100
Those are the Alps.

342
00:31:53,560 --> 00:31:55,228
Yes, I can see that.

343
00:31:55,311 --> 00:31:57,230
And their train took this route...

344
00:31:57,313 --> 00:31:59,357
- ...from the Nazi stronghold in 1944.
- Yes.

345
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:01,317
It crossed this mountain pass...

346
00:32:01,401 --> 00:32:02,986
...and went this way.

347
00:32:03,069 --> 00:32:05,363
- Here, the only river on the route.
- Yes.

348
00:32:05,446 --> 00:32:06,614
Under a bridge.

349
00:32:06,698 --> 00:32:08,157
It has to be down there.

350
00:32:08,241 --> 00:32:10,660
- And only we know about it.
- "We"?

351
00:32:10,952 --> 00:32:12,537
I mean, you.

352
00:32:12,620 --> 00:32:14,247
And me. Yes.

353
00:32:14,330 --> 00:32:16,165
- Us.
- Us.

354
00:32:17,166 --> 00:32:19,669
And what were you thinking?

355
00:32:21,129 --> 00:32:22,213
That maybe...

356
00:32:24,048 --> 00:32:25,675
...we could go there.

357
00:32:25,758 --> 00:32:26,885
And?

358
00:32:26,968 --> 00:32:28,052
And find it.

359
00:32:28,970 --> 00:32:30,597
And I'd become...

360
00:32:32,223 --> 00:32:33,474
...famous.

361
00:32:33,558 --> 00:32:34,934
Not famous.

362
00:32:35,018 --> 00:32:35,852
Renowned. Respected.

363
00:32:36,436 --> 00:32:38,313
A prestigious archaeologist.

364
00:32:38,396 --> 00:32:40,231
And you'd have one last triumph!

365
00:32:40,315 --> 00:32:41,316
Indiana Jones!

366
00:32:41,399 --> 00:32:43,443
You'd go out with a bang!
You'd get back in the saddle!

367
00:32:46,487 --> 00:32:47,906
You're not convinced, are you?

368
00:32:50,408 --> 00:32:51,618
Wombat...

369
00:32:53,745 --> 00:32:55,580
...why are you chasing the thing...

370
00:32:57,332 --> 00:32:59,417
...that drove your father mad?

371
00:33:05,381 --> 00:33:06,799
Wouldn't you?

372
00:33:25,151 --> 00:33:26,069
I can.

373
00:33:26,152 --> 00:33:27,111
The parade is coming.

374
00:33:28,321 --> 00:33:29,155
What have we got?

375
00:33:30,907 --> 00:33:33,368
She's on the third floor with an old man.

376
00:33:33,451 --> 00:33:34,661
With a Russian?

377
00:33:34,744 --> 00:33:36,663
No, a professor. Dr. Henry Jones.

378
00:33:37,914 --> 00:33:38,748
Klaber!

379
00:33:39,249 --> 00:33:41,417
Get back here! You're not an agent!

380
00:33:41,501 --> 00:33:42,877
Damn it! Go.

381
00:33:42,961 --> 00:33:45,547
I'll get backup
and pull a file on Jones.

382
00:34:06,192 --> 00:34:07,443
This way.

383
00:34:43,604 --> 00:34:46,691
Basil became obsessed
with this German theory.

384
00:34:48,234 --> 00:34:49,611
It's a conjecture.

385
00:34:52,155 --> 00:34:54,532
Archimedes had discovered...

386
00:34:54,615 --> 00:34:56,868
...that the movements
of the moon and planets...

387
00:34:57,744 --> 00:34:58,953
...weren't perfect.

388
00:35:00,205 --> 00:35:02,707
There were irregularities in their orbits.

389
00:35:03,875 --> 00:35:06,252
He thought these irregularities...

390
00:35:06,336 --> 00:35:09,714
...might explain changes
in temperature, the tides.

391
00:35:11,841 --> 00:35:13,134
Even storms.

392
00:35:14,510 --> 00:35:17,388
So he began building
a device to predict them.

393
00:35:19,098 --> 00:35:23,019
But he stumbled on a method
for predicting greater...

394
00:35:23,770 --> 00:35:24,771
...disturbances.

395
00:35:26,648 --> 00:35:28,066
Greater disturbances?

396
00:35:28,691 --> 00:35:30,902
Your father thought this thing...

397
00:35:31,611 --> 00:35:33,863
...could predict fissures in time.

398
00:35:50,880 --> 00:35:52,382
Hauke, if you don't...

399
00:35:54,801 --> 00:35:55,885
I found them.

400
00:36:04,561 --> 00:36:05,478
Hello.

401
00:36:06,688 --> 00:36:08,982
Are you looking for...

402
00:36:09,065 --> 00:36:10,233
...Dr. Jones?

403
00:36:13,903 --> 00:36:15,154
Can I help you?

404
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:16,739
No, thank you.

405
00:36:16,823 --> 00:36:18,533
It's a routine investigation.

406
00:36:19,617 --> 00:36:22,203
Are you police?

407
00:36:22,287 --> 00:36:23,705
It'll only take a moment.

408
00:36:23,788 --> 00:36:25,707
- Professor Plimpton.
- Ma'am.

409
00:36:25,790 --> 00:36:26,916
- Professor Plimpton!
- Ma'am!

410
00:36:31,462 --> 00:36:32,297
Drop your gun.

411
00:36:32,672 --> 00:36:33,673
What the hell are you doing?

412
00:36:34,966 --> 00:36:37,135
What the doctor tells me, Miss Mason.

413
00:36:37,218 --> 00:36:38,094
Mandy?

414
00:36:42,849 --> 00:36:44,893
Yes, no witnesses.

415
00:36:49,272 --> 00:36:51,357
Good God. What the hell?

416
00:36:51,649 --> 00:36:54,944
Your father wrote so many letters
about the dial that I stopped reading them.

417
00:36:58,072 --> 00:37:00,783
You really don't remember
the last time I came to your house?

418
00:37:02,744 --> 00:37:04,621
That's when I took it away from him.

419
00:37:06,247 --> 00:37:08,041
I thought he'd stopped thinking about it.

420
00:37:08,124 --> 00:37:12,086
But your father was convinced
that thing was real and dangerous.

421
00:37:12,170 --> 00:37:14,130
Don't shoot.

422
00:37:14,547 --> 00:37:18,051
He was terrified someone would find
a legendary tablet...

423
00:37:18,134 --> 00:37:19,344
...the Grafikos...

424
00:37:19,427 --> 00:37:21,679
...the tablet that tells you
how to find the rest of the dial.

425
00:37:21,763 --> 00:37:22,972
With the Grafikos...

426
00:37:23,056 --> 00:37:24,682
...they could get the rest of the dial...

427
00:37:24,766 --> 00:37:25,600
...and join the halves together.

428
00:37:27,268 --> 00:37:28,478
I knew you wouldn't destroy it.

429
00:37:32,106 --> 00:37:33,942
How do you know
he asked me to destroy it?

430
00:37:34,442 --> 00:37:35,360
What?

431
00:37:35,443 --> 00:37:36,819
You remember that night.

432
00:37:38,238 --> 00:37:40,990
- I was twelve, Indy.
- You knew we didn't throw it in the river.

433
00:37:41,824 --> 00:37:42,992
Baz didn't tell you that.

434
00:37:43,076 --> 00:37:45,078
- Look, I didn't...
- No, he never lied.

435
00:37:45,620 --> 00:37:48,081
- No, you...
- What was that nonsense about the map?

436
00:37:48,164 --> 00:37:49,207
No, you've had too much whiskey.

437
00:37:50,083 --> 00:37:51,084
What are you doing, Wombat?

438
00:37:51,167 --> 00:37:52,335
Stay right there.

439
00:37:53,878 --> 00:37:54,712
Don't move.

440
00:37:55,463 --> 00:37:57,006
- Who are they?
- Let's get out of here.

441
00:37:57,090 --> 00:37:58,007
Are they with you?

442
00:37:58,091 --> 00:37:59,259
Hold still, Miss Shaw.

443
00:38:00,593 --> 00:38:01,594
Miss Shaw, stop!

444
00:38:04,973 --> 00:38:05,807
Helena!

445
00:38:11,229 --> 00:38:12,063
Stop!

446
00:38:14,023 --> 00:38:15,149
Sorry.

447
00:38:15,233 --> 00:38:16,109
Helena!

448
00:38:16,192 --> 00:38:18,152
Dr. Jones. It's over.

449
00:38:18,236 --> 00:38:19,529
Who are they?

450
00:38:19,612 --> 00:38:20,697
What do they want?

451
00:38:22,699 --> 00:38:23,533
Stop!

452
00:38:25,702 --> 00:38:26,619
Helena Shaw!

453
00:38:26,703 --> 00:38:28,037
She's on the roof. Go up there.

454
00:38:28,121 --> 00:38:28,955
Dr. Jones!

455
00:38:29,789 --> 00:38:31,165
We won't hurt you.

456
00:38:57,692 --> 00:39:00,195
- Quick. We have to follow them.
- Hurry.

457
00:39:27,764 --> 00:39:28,598
Split up!

458
00:39:41,778 --> 00:39:44,239
- Quick! Pick it up!
- Take everything.

459
00:39:44,322 --> 00:39:45,448
Find her!

460
00:40:13,893 --> 00:40:16,271
- Operator.
- I need the police.

461
00:40:16,354 --> 00:40:18,731
Hunter College. There are dead people.

462
00:40:18,815 --> 00:40:19,899
- Please...
- Hang up.

463
00:40:25,780 --> 00:40:26,698
Stand up.

464
00:40:32,328 --> 00:40:33,288
All right.

465
00:40:57,770 --> 00:40:59,314
She's gone. You ruined it...

466
00:40:59,397 --> 00:41:00,732
...you trigger-happy white boy.

467
00:41:00,815 --> 00:41:03,276
- She had it in her hand.
- We have the professor.

468
00:41:06,863 --> 00:41:08,114
Jones's file.

469
00:41:08,198 --> 00:41:09,115
Yes.

470
00:41:27,383 --> 00:41:28,509
Who are you?

471
00:41:28,593 --> 00:41:30,053
That's my question.

472
00:41:40,563 --> 00:41:42,106
They're CIA.

473
00:41:42,190 --> 00:41:44,025
Not me, man.

474
00:41:44,108 --> 00:41:46,152
I don't take government jobs.

475
00:41:46,236 --> 00:41:47,070
All right.

476
00:41:48,613 --> 00:41:50,782
How do you know Miss Shaw?

477
00:41:51,157 --> 00:41:52,408
She's my goddaughter.

478
00:41:53,159 --> 00:41:54,869
I haven't seen her in eighteen years.

479
00:41:54,953 --> 00:41:57,121
Why did you see her today? To give her the dial?

480
00:41:58,498 --> 00:42:00,959
It's an ancient geared mechanism.

481
00:42:01,918 --> 00:42:02,961
Half a mechanism.

482
00:42:03,044 --> 00:42:04,754
It's much more than that.

483
00:42:06,464 --> 00:42:07,632
Stop!

484
00:42:07,715 --> 00:42:09,050
Where do you think you're going?

485
00:42:09,133 --> 00:42:10,593
There's a parade.

486
00:42:10,677 --> 00:42:12,220
And a protest is coming.

487
00:42:12,303 --> 00:42:13,346
Hey!

488
00:42:14,013 --> 00:42:15,139
- Hey...
- Shut up.

489
00:42:15,223 --> 00:42:17,642
I can't go this way. I have to turn back.

490
00:42:22,647 --> 00:42:24,482
No! What's wrong with you, old man?

491
00:42:24,566 --> 00:42:26,276
Damn it! We're going on foot.

492
00:42:26,359 --> 00:42:27,193
You heard her.

493
00:42:29,237 --> 00:42:30,196
What were you thinking?

494
00:42:30,280 --> 00:42:31,656
- Walk! Come on.
- What's wrong with you?

495
00:42:31,739 --> 00:42:33,324
- Don't you look when you back up?
- Deal with him.

496
00:42:33,408 --> 00:42:34,284
Who's going to pay for this?

497
00:42:34,367 --> 00:42:35,618
Easy, buddy.

498
00:42:35,702 --> 00:42:36,536
Walk.

499
00:42:36,619 --> 00:42:38,413
Can't you see my cab? It's yellow.

500
00:42:38,496 --> 00:42:40,498
No, you're paying for the repairs.

501
00:42:43,042 --> 00:42:45,336
Peace now!

502
00:42:46,754 --> 00:42:49,674
Stop the war! Stop fighting!

503
00:42:49,757 --> 00:42:51,509
- Where are we going, Mason?
- This way.

504
00:42:53,553 --> 00:42:55,221
- Hell no, we won't go.
- Shut up.

505
00:42:55,305 --> 00:42:56,723
- Hell no, we won't go!
- Hell no!

506
00:42:57,056 --> 00:42:59,726
- Hell no, we won't go!
- Hell no, we won't go!

507
00:42:59,809 --> 00:43:00,768
We won't go!

508
00:43:00,852 --> 00:43:03,229
- Hell no, we won't go!
- Hell no, we won't go!

509
00:43:03,813 --> 00:43:05,231
- Hell no...
- Shut up!

510
00:43:05,565 --> 00:43:06,399
Hell yes!

511
00:43:16,451 --> 00:43:17,452
Hell no...

512
00:43:28,379 --> 00:43:29,214
Hey!

513
00:43:30,131 --> 00:43:32,133
Officer! Help!

514
00:43:32,884 --> 00:43:34,469
Officer, there was a shooting...

515
00:43:34,552 --> 00:43:36,471
- All right.
- ...this morning at Hunter.

516
00:43:36,554 --> 00:43:38,389
There are dead people. Damn it.

517
00:43:38,473 --> 00:43:41,893
- Some crazy thugs burst in...
- Sir, calm down.

518
00:43:41,976 --> 00:43:43,269
Please listen to me.

519
00:43:43,353 --> 00:43:45,563
There was a shooting this morning, and...

520
00:43:45,647 --> 00:43:49,067
They're in a Con Ed van,
just around the corner!

521
00:43:51,653 --> 00:43:53,279
Hey! What do you think you're doing?

522
00:44:05,458 --> 00:44:06,793
- Watch out!
- What are you doing?

523
00:44:27,605 --> 00:44:28,690
They made it!

524
00:44:56,009 --> 00:44:57,010
Get out of the way!

525
00:45:05,184 --> 00:45:06,019
Watch out!

526
00:45:08,354 --> 00:45:09,522
Move! Quickly!

527
00:45:24,287 --> 00:45:25,205
Out of the way!

528
00:45:35,798 --> 00:45:37,175
Hurry!

529
00:45:57,779 --> 00:46:00,532
Come on. This is ridiculous. It's a horse.

530
00:46:00,615 --> 00:46:01,449
Come on!

531
00:46:03,952 --> 00:46:05,787
- Hey, sir.
- Move aside!

532
00:46:06,120 --> 00:46:07,038
Hold my horse.

533
00:46:07,121 --> 00:46:08,331
Move!

534
00:46:08,414 --> 00:46:11,125
Transfer available
to the IND Queens Boulevard line.

535
00:46:20,593 --> 00:46:23,596
Next stop,
59th Street–Lexington Avenue.

536
00:46:25,473 --> 00:46:26,724
The subway is faster.

537
00:46:28,268 --> 00:46:32,146
People romanticize science,
though it's quite cold.

538
00:46:32,230 --> 00:46:33,731
What's next, Dr. Schmidt?

539
00:46:34,858 --> 00:46:35,775
Mars?

540
00:46:36,734 --> 00:46:38,152
No, we've conquered space.

541
00:46:39,988 --> 00:46:41,698
I'll move on to the next frontier.

542
00:46:43,157 --> 00:46:45,577
What's beyond space?

543
00:46:49,205 --> 00:46:52,208
You'd better
iron your suit, Dr. Schmidt.

544
00:46:52,834 --> 00:46:54,460
We're going to the airport in an hour.

545
00:46:54,836 --> 00:46:56,004
You'll meet the president.

546
00:46:56,087 --> 00:46:57,672
If a few wrinkles bother him...

547
00:46:57,755 --> 00:47:00,008
...perhaps he should find another physicist.

548
00:47:01,009 --> 00:47:02,844
- Can I quote you on that?
- No.

549
00:47:03,219 --> 00:47:04,053
Yes.

550
00:47:05,805 --> 00:47:06,639
Baxter.

551
00:47:10,602 --> 00:47:11,728
It's for you, Doctor.

552
00:47:15,481 --> 00:47:17,609
I may go to Los Angeles later.

553
00:47:17,692 --> 00:47:19,944
I'm expecting a delivery soon.

554
00:47:23,198 --> 00:47:24,115
Speak.

555
00:47:24,199 --> 00:47:25,783
Your thugs made a mess.

556
00:47:25,867 --> 00:47:27,118
Did they?

557
00:47:27,202 --> 00:47:29,621
Miss Shaw
met with Professor Jones.

558
00:47:30,246 --> 00:47:31,998
He gave her the device.

559
00:47:32,916 --> 00:47:34,000
And we lost her.

560
00:47:35,668 --> 00:47:37,337
Dr. Jones escaped too.

561
00:47:37,754 --> 00:47:39,005
Really?

562
00:47:39,088 --> 00:47:40,423
That's very unfortunate.

563
00:47:40,798 --> 00:47:42,467
I have to clean everything up here, Doctor.

564
00:47:42,550 --> 00:47:44,886
And as a government representative...

565
00:47:44,969 --> 00:47:46,763
...I urge you to cooperate.

566
00:47:46,846 --> 00:47:48,056
Take that plane to Los Angeles...

567
00:47:48,973 --> 00:47:50,225
...and accept the president's medal.

568
00:47:57,857 --> 00:47:58,691
Hello?

569
00:47:59,442 --> 00:48:01,611
Call our friends
and get a private charter.

570
00:48:03,029 --> 00:48:04,030
To Morocco.

571
00:48:04,113 --> 00:48:05,281
Yes, sir.

572
00:48:06,199 --> 00:48:09,494
The murders at the university
happened in the middle of the parade.

573
00:48:09,577 --> 00:48:13,081
Police are searching for Dr. Henry Jones,
a retired professor.

574
00:48:13,164 --> 00:48:16,251
A colleague told ABC
that Jones recently lost his son...

575
00:48:16,334 --> 00:48:18,336
...and was in the middle of a divorce.

576
00:48:21,047 --> 00:48:23,758
That guy looks like you.

577
00:48:24,717 --> 00:48:27,095
No.

578
00:48:29,055 --> 00:48:30,139
It is you.

579
00:48:30,223 --> 00:48:31,140
No. Hey.

580
00:48:31,224 --> 00:48:32,809
- Go home. You're drunk.
- It's him!

581
00:48:32,892 --> 00:48:34,185
- Hey!
- He's the murderer!

582
00:48:36,855 --> 00:48:38,356
Sorry I'm late, Indy.

583
00:48:38,439 --> 00:48:39,858
Traffic on the bridge.

584
00:48:50,743 --> 00:48:52,912
Good to see you, Sallah.

585
00:48:53,746 --> 00:48:55,748
Wish I could say the same, old friend.

586
00:48:59,043 --> 00:49:00,962
Quick, Indy. Get in.

587
00:49:04,757 --> 00:49:07,135
Your goddaughter, Helena...

588
00:49:07,218 --> 00:49:09,888
...was arrested in Tangier last year...

589
00:49:10,805 --> 00:49:12,807
...for auctioning contraband.

590
00:49:15,268 --> 00:49:16,227
And there's more.

591
00:49:17,395 --> 00:49:20,815
Aziz Rahim paid her bail.

592
00:49:21,566 --> 00:49:24,485
He's the son of Big Rahim...

593
00:49:24,569 --> 00:49:27,322
...a well-known Moroccan mobster.

594
00:49:28,865 --> 00:49:32,619
Big Rahim
owns the Hotel Atlantique...

595
00:49:32,702 --> 00:49:35,371
...in Tangier, where this week...

596
00:49:35,455 --> 00:49:39,667
...they're holding the annual auction...

597
00:49:39,751 --> 00:49:41,628
...of stolen antiquities.

598
00:49:42,795 --> 00:49:45,381
All the big players are already there.

599
00:49:45,465 --> 00:49:47,342
Alia, Jabari.

600
00:49:47,425 --> 00:49:51,679
He's the great man who brought our
family to the U.S. during the war.

601
00:49:51,763 --> 00:49:54,140
Quick, when was the Suez Crisis?

602
00:49:55,016 --> 00:49:56,684
In 1956.

603
00:49:57,143 --> 00:49:58,603
Very impressive, Jabari.

604
00:49:58,686 --> 00:50:01,189
My grandchildren watch too much TV...

605
00:50:01,272 --> 00:50:03,900
...but they know their history.

606
00:50:03,983 --> 00:50:08,112
They understand what it means
to be American and Egyptian.

607
00:50:08,988 --> 00:50:11,157
Take me to the airport, Sallah.

608
00:50:12,242 --> 00:50:14,744
If you run,
the police will think you're guilty.

609
00:50:15,453 --> 00:50:18,790
Without Helena or the dial,
they'll frame me for murder.

610
00:50:19,958 --> 00:50:21,584
Have you thought about calling Marion?

611
00:50:22,669 --> 00:50:24,003
She won't talk to me.

612
00:50:30,176 --> 00:50:33,763
Welcome to New York's
John F. Kennedy International Airport.

613
00:50:34,597 --> 00:50:36,975
The upper level is
for Pan American passengers.

614
00:50:37,058 --> 00:50:38,977
I took something else from your apartment.

615
00:50:39,060 --> 00:50:40,270
It was under the bed.

616
00:50:45,483 --> 00:50:46,526
Thank you, Sallah.

617
00:50:48,778 --> 00:50:50,572
I brought my passport too.

618
00:50:52,615 --> 00:50:53,908
I could help you.

619
00:50:53,992 --> 00:50:54,993
In Tangier?

620
00:50:55,076 --> 00:50:56,703
Wherever chance takes us.

621
00:50:57,620 --> 00:50:58,705
Indy, I...

622
00:50:59,622 --> 00:51:01,082
I miss the desert.

623
00:51:01,666 --> 00:51:02,959
The sea.

624
00:51:04,502 --> 00:51:06,379
And waking up in the morning...

625
00:51:06,462 --> 00:51:09,591
...wondering what adventure
the new day will bring.

626
00:51:10,550 --> 00:51:12,552
This isn't an adventure, Sallah.

627
00:51:14,012 --> 00:51:16,681
Those days are over.

628
00:51:16,764 --> 00:51:17,891
Maybe.

629
00:51:17,974 --> 00:51:19,309
Maybe not.

630
00:51:23,771 --> 00:51:26,399
Make them suffer, Indiana Jones!

631
00:51:37,994 --> 00:51:38,870
Champagne?

632
00:51:39,454 --> 00:51:41,331
Four hours to Tangier.

633
00:51:49,088 --> 00:51:50,506
Your whiskey, sir.

634
00:51:51,132 --> 00:51:52,133
Thank you.

635
00:51:59,057 --> 00:52:00,516
Basil. Come on, Baz!

636
00:52:00,600 --> 00:52:02,435
- Open the door!
- No!

637
00:52:02,518 --> 00:52:04,020
- Don't come in, Indy!
- Open up.

638
00:52:07,232 --> 00:52:09,108
The Germans were right.

639
00:52:09,776 --> 00:52:11,653
- What?
- It's very important.

640
00:52:11,736 --> 00:52:12,654
Hey, Ba... Hey!

641
00:52:12,737 --> 00:52:14,614
- Hey, Baz! Give me...
- No.

642
00:52:14,697 --> 00:52:16,866
- What are you doing?
- You didn't listen to me.

643
00:52:16,950 --> 00:52:18,743
I don't understand, Baz!

644
00:52:19,077 --> 00:52:21,120
I tried to explain downstairs.

645
00:52:21,204 --> 00:52:23,498
- You ignored everything.
- You're terrifying your daughter.

646
00:52:25,667 --> 00:52:27,835
Indy, Archimedes...

647
00:52:27,919 --> 00:52:30,129
...discovered temporal meteorology.

648
00:52:30,338 --> 00:52:33,049
He was a mathematician, Baz...

649
00:52:33,132 --> 00:52:34,425
...not a magician.

650
00:52:34,509 --> 00:52:37,053
He could predict fissures in time.

651
00:52:37,136 --> 00:52:38,680
Fissures in time?

652
00:52:39,055 --> 00:52:42,183
- Baz, you can't prove that!
- Not yet.

653
00:52:42,267 --> 00:52:44,143
Proving it makes it science!

654
00:52:59,409 --> 00:53:00,618
Baz.

655
00:53:02,328 --> 00:53:03,872
I never should've given you that.

656
00:53:04,581 --> 00:53:06,082
It belongs in a museum.

657
00:53:07,000 --> 00:53:08,710
Give it to me. Please.

658
00:53:12,297 --> 00:53:13,673
If I give it to you...

659
00:53:14,883 --> 00:53:16,342
...you have to destroy it.

660
00:53:17,844 --> 00:53:18,720
I will.

661
00:53:21,556 --> 00:53:22,974
I'll destroy it, Baz.

662
00:53:25,476 --> 00:53:26,477
Promise me.

663
00:53:32,609 --> 00:53:34,194
I'm sorry about all this.

664
00:53:34,277 --> 00:53:35,486
It's my fault.

665
00:53:37,697 --> 00:53:39,157
- Are you staying?
- Yes.

666
00:53:39,240 --> 00:53:41,409
All right. I have a flight to catch.

667
00:53:41,492 --> 00:53:44,370
If someone finds the Grafikos,
they'll have the whole thing.

668
00:53:44,454 --> 00:53:45,872
Some things should stay buried.

669
00:53:45,955 --> 00:53:46,789
I know, Baz.

670
00:53:46,873 --> 00:53:48,750
- It's vital that you destroy it.
- I will.

671
00:53:48,833 --> 00:53:50,293
- Do you understand?
- I'll destroy it.

672
00:53:50,376 --> 00:53:51,211
- Say it.
- Yes.

673
00:53:51,294 --> 00:53:52,378
I will... Yes.

674
00:53:52,462 --> 00:53:54,130
You promised me. Remember that.

675
00:53:54,214 --> 00:53:55,298
I promise.

676
00:53:55,381 --> 00:53:56,341
There's a reason...

677
00:53:56,424 --> 00:53:59,093
...Archimedes split it in two.

678
00:53:59,177 --> 00:54:00,970
I know, Baz.

679
00:54:01,054 --> 00:54:01,930
Indy.

680
00:54:03,932 --> 00:54:05,308
Thank you, Wombat.

681
00:54:05,391 --> 00:54:07,352
He'll be fine in a few days.

682
00:54:09,437 --> 00:54:10,855
I'll call as soon as I land.

683
00:54:15,944 --> 00:54:17,278
Ladies and gentlemen...

684
00:54:17,362 --> 00:54:19,822
...we'll be landing in Tangier in twenty minutes.

685
00:54:53,606 --> 00:54:55,066
- Don't drink!
- What are you doing?

686
00:54:55,149 --> 00:54:56,818
Enough champagne! How much...?

687
00:54:56,901 --> 00:54:57,861
I'll stay.

688
00:54:57,944 --> 00:54:59,404
Go home!

689
00:55:16,087 --> 00:55:18,423
Miss Shaw is here.

690
00:55:22,677 --> 00:55:23,511
It's bronze.

691
00:55:23,928 --> 00:55:25,054
Almost complete.

692
00:55:25,138 --> 00:55:26,764
It's an astrological clock.

693
00:55:27,348 --> 00:55:29,642
It dates from the third century BC...

694
00:55:29,726 --> 00:55:33,646
...and was built by Archimedes himself.

695
00:55:35,690 --> 00:55:37,192
Let's start at twenty thousand.

696
00:55:37,275 --> 00:55:38,443
Twenty thousand, thank you very much.

697
00:55:38,526 --> 00:55:39,944
Thirty.

698
00:55:40,028 --> 00:55:40,945
Green gauges...

699
00:55:41,487 --> 00:55:42,780
...full power...

700
00:55:42,864 --> 00:55:45,200
...and when I hit 85 miles an hour, I rotate.

701
00:55:45,825 --> 00:55:46,743
And now what?

702
00:55:46,826 --> 00:55:48,995
- Help him, Louis! He's adorable.
- Pull on the yoke.

703
00:55:49,078 --> 00:55:51,372
- I did. I'm flying.
- Yes.

704
00:55:51,456 --> 00:55:52,749
- Let me have a sip.
- Should I raise the flaps?

705
00:55:52,832 --> 00:55:54,918
Don't touch them below four hundred feet.

706
00:55:55,001 --> 00:55:56,753
Lower the nose to reach 120 miles an hour.

707
00:55:56,836 --> 00:55:57,837
All right.

708
00:56:00,423 --> 00:56:02,759
- Private auction, old-timer.
- I need to get in.

709
00:56:02,842 --> 00:56:05,345
No password, no entry.
I don't make the rules.

710
00:56:08,181 --> 00:56:10,016
- What's the current bid?
- Fifty thousand.

711
00:56:10,099 --> 00:56:11,351
Then I bid fifty-five.

712
00:56:12,310 --> 00:56:13,394
- Who'll give sixty?
- Sixty.

713
00:56:13,478 --> 00:56:15,063
Sixty. Sixty-five?

714
00:56:15,146 --> 00:56:16,397
- Sixty-five. Seventy?
- Seventy-five.

715
00:56:16,481 --> 00:56:17,815
- Seventy-five. Eighty?
- Eighty.

716
00:56:17,899 --> 00:56:19,526
- Eighty-five.
- How about ninety?

717
00:56:26,533 --> 00:56:27,367
It's a private auction.

718
00:56:27,700 --> 00:56:28,785
The auction is over.

719
00:56:29,202 --> 00:56:30,578
Au contraire, it's just begun.

720
00:56:30,662 --> 00:56:32,163
- One hundred.
- One hundred thousand.

721
00:56:32,247 --> 00:56:33,873
I like the hat.

722
00:56:33,957 --> 00:56:35,291
It makes you look two years younger.

723
00:56:35,375 --> 00:56:36,543
Thank you.

724
00:56:36,626 --> 00:56:38,294
- One hundred ten. Bravo.
- I said it's over.

725
00:56:38,378 --> 00:56:39,754
Who is he?

726
00:56:39,837 --> 00:56:41,297
- Her godfather.
- A sort of relative.

727
00:56:41,381 --> 00:56:43,383
And she should be asleep.

728
00:56:43,466 --> 00:56:44,759
- Come on, Wombat.
- I wouldn't do that.

729
00:56:44,842 --> 00:56:46,427
Will you explain it to the police at the bar?

730
00:56:46,511 --> 00:56:47,845
The ones I bribed?

731
00:56:48,388 --> 00:56:50,056
You're out of your depth, Jonesy.

732
00:56:50,139 --> 00:56:51,558
- "Jonesy"?
- He thinks I'm a criminal.

733
00:56:51,641 --> 00:56:53,476
He's wanted for murder.

734
00:56:53,560 --> 00:56:55,687
Nice picture in the New York Herald. One thirty?

735
00:56:55,770 --> 00:56:57,480
I didn't kill anyone.

736
00:56:57,564 --> 00:56:59,357
- You can't come through.
- And you know it.

737
00:57:00,233 --> 00:57:03,403
But the man who did was after this.

738
00:57:04,070 --> 00:57:05,947
It's Pandora's box.

739
00:57:07,198 --> 00:57:08,408
Actually, no.

740
00:57:09,284 --> 00:57:10,743
It's my box.

741
00:57:13,371 --> 00:57:14,372
You.

742
00:57:16,082 --> 00:57:17,208
Have we met?

743
00:57:17,542 --> 00:57:18,459
No.

744
00:57:18,543 --> 00:57:20,003
My memory is a little fuzzy...

745
00:57:20,086 --> 00:57:21,796
...but you look familiar.

746
00:57:22,422 --> 00:57:23,464
Are you still a Nazi?

747
00:57:27,552 --> 00:57:29,721
You're mistaken. My name is Schmidt.

748
00:57:30,305 --> 00:57:32,473
Professor Schmidt
from the University of Alabama.

749
00:57:34,183 --> 00:57:35,101
Professor Schmidt.

750
00:57:35,560 --> 00:57:36,519
It's a pleasure to meet you in person.

751
00:57:37,604 --> 00:57:39,355
- One fifty.
- After our conversation...

752
00:57:39,439 --> 00:57:41,482
...I thought we had an agreement
about the dial.

753
00:57:41,566 --> 00:57:43,860
Strange. When I saw the man who looks like you...

754
00:57:43,943 --> 00:57:45,695
...he was after this too.

755
00:57:46,279 --> 00:57:47,989
He offered too little, Professor.

756
00:57:48,072 --> 00:57:49,240
Good thing you're here.

757
00:57:49,324 --> 00:57:51,868
- We have a bid of one sixty.
- You don't understand, Miss Shaw.

758
00:57:51,951 --> 00:57:53,286
This artifact belongs to me.

759
00:57:53,369 --> 00:57:54,954
No, it doesn't. You stole it.

760
00:57:55,038 --> 00:57:55,872
And then you did.

761
00:57:55,955 --> 00:57:57,624
And then I did. It's called capitalism.

762
00:57:57,707 --> 00:57:58,666
We have a bid of one sixty.

763
00:57:58,750 --> 00:57:59,709
- One sixty.
- One seventy?

764
00:57:59,792 --> 00:58:00,835
You should never have left New York.

765
00:58:00,919 --> 00:58:02,879
- One seventy?
- You should never have gone to Poland.

766
00:58:02,962 --> 00:58:04,881
One seventy. Anyone?

767
00:58:04,964 --> 00:58:06,257
Going, going...

768
00:58:06,799 --> 00:58:07,634
Gone!

769
00:58:08,259 --> 00:58:09,260
Stay back!

770
00:58:11,012 --> 00:58:12,013
Take the dial.

771
00:58:16,851 --> 00:58:17,685
Stay back!

772
00:58:26,986 --> 00:58:27,987
Hello, Claude.

773
00:58:28,071 --> 00:58:29,447
You shouldn't have come back, Helena.

774
00:58:38,540 --> 00:58:39,832
Back off.

775
00:58:50,718 --> 00:58:51,553
Teddy!

776
00:59:05,942 --> 00:59:07,068
Rahim says you have to stay.

777
00:59:19,038 --> 00:59:19,873
Thank you.

778
00:59:32,969 --> 00:59:34,429
See you in the past, Dr. Jones.

779
00:59:42,437 --> 00:59:44,189
Move aside!

780
01:00:01,497 --> 01:00:02,498
That's my taxi!

781
01:00:08,046 --> 01:00:09,297
Tell them to leave.

782
01:00:09,380 --> 01:00:10,757
I told them to shoot you.

783
01:00:12,842 --> 01:00:14,052
All right.

784
01:00:15,428 --> 01:00:16,888
Lower your guns.

785
01:00:16,971 --> 01:00:18,181
Put them down.

786
01:00:20,308 --> 01:00:21,142
Good.

787
01:00:22,810 --> 01:00:24,145
That's better.

788
01:00:27,982 --> 01:00:29,609
Good heavens.

789
01:00:39,661 --> 01:00:40,787
Rahim.

790
01:00:41,871 --> 01:00:43,373
You're in pajamas.

791
01:00:43,873 --> 01:00:45,583
I was sleeping, Helena.

792
01:00:46,042 --> 01:00:46,960
Peacefully.

793
01:00:47,877 --> 01:00:49,796
Then my father woke me.

794
01:00:49,879 --> 01:00:52,507
He told me you'd come back to our hotel.

795
01:00:54,008 --> 01:00:56,344
Then he gave me this scimitar...

796
01:00:56,427 --> 01:00:59,264
...and told me
to come home with your head.

797
01:01:01,099 --> 01:01:02,392
Does it have to be that part of my body?

798
01:01:10,149 --> 01:01:13,027
I thought maybe you came back because you loved me.

799
01:01:13,111 --> 01:01:14,237
Rahim...

800
01:01:14,988 --> 01:01:16,656
I needed to sell an object.

801
01:01:16,739 --> 01:01:18,700
Sorry. The object you're talking about...

802
01:01:18,783 --> 01:01:20,493
- ...belongs to me.
- Don't get involved...

803
01:01:20,577 --> 01:01:21,744
- And some bad people...
- ...in this.

804
01:01:21,828 --> 01:01:24,038
- ...very bad people took it.
- Don't interfere...

805
01:01:24,122 --> 01:01:25,123
- ...in my business.
- Is he the new one?

806
01:01:25,206 --> 01:01:26,499
- No.
- It's complicated.

807
01:01:28,251 --> 01:01:29,836
What do you owe this guy?

808
01:01:29,919 --> 01:01:32,088
The bail money
and a lifetime of happiness.

809
01:01:32,171 --> 01:01:33,923
You sold the ring, I suppose.

810
01:01:34,007 --> 01:01:35,341
I didn't get as much as I expected.

811
01:01:35,800 --> 01:01:36,926
I'll do it!

812
01:01:37,010 --> 01:01:38,052
What are you...? Hey!

813
01:01:44,350 --> 01:01:45,935
You got people trying to kill me!

814
01:01:46,019 --> 01:01:48,688
I'm not engaged to a mobster!

815
01:01:48,771 --> 01:01:51,774
I don't need moral lessons
from a tomb robber!

816
01:01:51,858 --> 01:01:53,151
I'm not a tomb robber.

817
01:01:53,234 --> 01:01:55,528
I did important work with your father.

818
01:01:57,906 --> 01:02:00,241
Were your adventures really selfless?

819
01:02:00,325 --> 01:02:01,201
It was for the thrill!

820
01:02:03,912 --> 01:02:04,954
Nice one, Teddy!

821
01:02:06,456 --> 01:02:08,166
- Move over, kid.
- Hey!

822
01:02:18,843 --> 01:02:20,720
- Helena!
- Reverse!

823
01:02:38,446 --> 01:02:39,405
Not this way!

824
01:02:39,489 --> 01:02:40,865
You don't know where you're going, Indy!

825
01:02:40,949 --> 01:02:42,784
- Listen to him! He knows the city!
- I know Tangier!

826
01:02:47,288 --> 01:02:48,331
There it is!

827
01:02:53,461 --> 01:02:55,672
Go faster!

828
01:02:55,755 --> 01:02:57,715
Ten minutes to the airport, boss.

829
01:02:59,676 --> 01:03:02,470
I don't know if Schmidt is a real professor.

830
01:03:02,554 --> 01:03:03,763
- He's a Nazi!
- Left!

831
01:03:03,846 --> 01:03:05,473
Left!

832
01:03:05,557 --> 01:03:06,641
- Oh, no!
- No!

833
01:03:21,322 --> 01:03:23,283
Helena! Don't do this!

834
01:03:31,249 --> 01:03:32,792
- This way!
- That way!

835
01:03:33,751 --> 01:03:34,586
No!

836
01:03:35,712 --> 01:03:37,213
Helena!

837
01:03:39,257 --> 01:03:40,258
No!

838
01:03:49,976 --> 01:03:51,519
What the hell?

839
01:03:58,151 --> 01:03:59,402
Helena!

840
01:04:01,362 --> 01:04:02,280
Hey!

841
01:04:02,363 --> 01:04:03,239
Hey!

842
01:04:03,823 --> 01:04:04,824
What the hell?

843
01:04:08,119 --> 01:04:09,162
Step on it!

844
01:04:12,957 --> 01:04:14,792
Come on!

845
01:04:48,660 --> 01:04:51,371
Do you think he'd be proud of this?

846
01:04:51,454 --> 01:04:52,372
Who?

847
01:04:54,123 --> 01:04:55,041
Your father!

848
01:04:55,124 --> 01:04:58,127
His only daughter selling her soul
for bail money!

849
01:04:58,211 --> 01:04:59,671
Sounds good when you put it like that.

850
01:04:59,754 --> 01:05:01,464
And it's not all bail money.

851
01:05:01,548 --> 01:05:03,925
- Some of it is gambling debt, and...
- Thanks, Teddy.

852
01:05:04,008 --> 01:05:05,802
Helena! No!

853
01:05:05,885 --> 01:05:06,886
Helena!

854
01:05:08,596 --> 01:05:10,557
How did you end up like this?

855
01:05:10,640 --> 01:05:12,058
Clever, you mean?

856
01:05:12,141 --> 01:05:14,477
Daring? Pretty? Self-sufficient?

857
01:05:39,794 --> 01:05:40,628
Hold on!

858
01:05:44,340 --> 01:05:45,341
Go this way!

859
01:05:54,893 --> 01:05:55,727
There it is.

860
01:06:08,448 --> 01:06:10,825
- Stop!
- Hey!

861
01:06:23,546 --> 01:06:24,714
Left!

862
01:06:25,840 --> 01:06:26,841
Take the wheel, Teddy!

863
01:06:29,052 --> 01:06:30,178
What are you doing?

864
01:06:30,261 --> 01:06:31,804
Taking back what's mine.

865
01:06:32,764 --> 01:06:33,765
Helena!

866
01:06:37,977 --> 01:06:39,520
Damn it! Are you crazy?

867
01:06:42,148 --> 01:06:44,067
Hey! Helena!

868
01:06:48,029 --> 01:06:49,656
- Take the gun!
- Miss Shaw.

869
01:06:51,074 --> 01:06:52,742
Let go, Miss Shaw!

870
01:07:15,598 --> 01:07:16,599
Let her go.

871
01:07:16,683 --> 01:07:19,060
Come on! Give me the dial!

872
01:07:20,562 --> 01:07:21,855
Helena!

873
01:07:23,773 --> 01:07:24,691
Damn it.

874
01:07:31,489 --> 01:07:33,658
- Turn around! They went that way!
- She's gone!

875
01:07:33,741 --> 01:07:35,577
- Move!
- Hey!

876
01:07:38,997 --> 01:07:42,125
I didn't come to rescue you from your fiancé!

877
01:07:42,208 --> 01:07:43,042
Rescue me?

878
01:07:48,214 --> 01:07:49,841
I want the dial back.

879
01:08:09,277 --> 01:08:10,778
Hands on your head.

880
01:08:12,030 --> 01:08:13,573
It wasn't my easiest breakup.

881
01:08:13,656 --> 01:08:15,950
- If your father were alive...
- He isn't, Indy.

882
01:08:16,034 --> 01:08:17,535
The engine's acting up.

883
01:08:17,619 --> 01:08:19,704
If I'd had a father figure...

884
01:08:19,786 --> 01:08:22,706
...someone appointed to that role.

885
01:08:22,790 --> 01:08:25,125
- You have no idea...
- Don't martyr yourself.

886
01:08:25,210 --> 01:08:27,295
What's a godfather?

887
01:08:28,295 --> 01:08:30,340
Family was never your strong suit.

888
01:08:31,007 --> 01:08:33,425
- By the way, what time is it?
- It's...

889
01:08:33,509 --> 01:08:34,509
That's not fair.

890
01:08:35,219 --> 01:08:36,262
Give it back.

891
01:08:36,346 --> 01:08:37,554
It was my father's watch!

892
01:08:40,099 --> 01:08:40,974
Give it back to him.

893
01:08:45,438 --> 01:08:46,940
You're leaving town with me.

894
01:08:47,023 --> 01:08:48,608
Rahim's looking for you too.

895
01:08:48,691 --> 01:08:49,983
They'll go to Tangier airport.

896
01:08:50,067 --> 01:08:52,237
We'll take a train
and then a plane in Casablanca.

897
01:08:54,405 --> 01:08:55,322
What was that?

898
01:09:00,578 --> 01:09:01,411
Damn it.

899
01:09:03,831 --> 01:09:04,999
Yes.

900
01:09:05,083 --> 01:09:05,959
I know, sir.

901
01:09:08,335 --> 01:09:09,212
Your...

902
01:09:10,588 --> 01:09:11,548
Understood.

903
01:09:16,678 --> 01:09:17,804
They've cut you off.

904
01:09:18,054 --> 01:09:19,429
You scared them.

905
01:09:19,806 --> 01:09:21,558
- Because they don't understand.
- No.

906
01:09:21,641 --> 01:09:24,143
Because your associates
killed three U.S. civilians...

907
01:09:24,227 --> 01:09:26,563
...and destroyed a televised parade.

908
01:09:26,646 --> 01:09:29,691
Because you stood up
the President of the United States...

909
01:09:29,773 --> 01:09:31,025
...fled to Morocco...

910
01:09:31,108 --> 01:09:33,486
...and caused an incident
requiring a military extraction!

911
01:09:35,612 --> 01:09:37,407
Take me to DC and I'll explain everything.

912
01:09:37,490 --> 01:09:38,907
They want me gone.

913
01:09:38,991 --> 01:09:41,201
Now we've got half the dial.

914
01:09:41,286 --> 01:09:42,620
They wanted to keep you happy...

915
01:09:43,621 --> 01:09:45,373
...by letting you look for it.

916
01:09:45,456 --> 01:09:46,749
They never cared.

917
01:09:46,833 --> 01:09:49,460
They'll care, Miss Mason,
when they understand what it does.

918
01:09:49,544 --> 01:09:50,670
You put them on the moon.

919
01:09:51,379 --> 01:09:52,630
They got what they wanted.

920
01:09:53,923 --> 01:09:55,842
Fasten your seat belts.
We'll be landing in Spain.

921
01:09:56,467 --> 01:09:58,553
A C-9 will take you to Maxwell.

922
01:09:58,636 --> 01:10:00,179
I'm not going back to Alabama!

923
01:10:00,263 --> 01:10:02,515
We need a boat
to reach the Mediterranean.

924
01:10:03,182 --> 01:10:04,642
The Grafikos will lead us to the other...

925
01:10:04,726 --> 01:10:06,853
Please, Miss Mason.

926
01:10:06,936 --> 01:10:08,062
Let me go, Schmidt.

927
01:10:08,146 --> 01:10:09,772
I'm asking you as a personal favor.

928
01:10:10,690 --> 01:10:12,108
You're a nasty piece of work, Doc.

929
01:10:13,818 --> 01:10:14,652
Hey!

930
01:10:42,680 --> 01:10:44,182
Schmidt...

931
01:10:45,099 --> 01:10:46,726
My name is Voller.

932
01:10:49,520 --> 01:10:50,897
Jürgen Voller.

933
01:11:04,160 --> 01:11:06,037
It's a Sea Stallion.

934
01:11:06,120 --> 01:11:07,539
An American helicopter.

935
01:11:08,540 --> 01:11:10,250
Did you know the Wright brothers?

936
01:11:10,708 --> 01:11:12,252
- What?
- The Wright brothers.

937
01:11:12,335 --> 01:11:13,503
Orville and Wilbur.

938
01:11:13,586 --> 01:11:15,922
They invented the airplane and lived in Indiana.

939
01:11:16,005 --> 01:11:17,632
Wilbur was born in Indiana.

940
01:11:17,715 --> 01:11:20,218
I'm not from Indiana, Teddy.

941
01:11:20,301 --> 01:11:22,929
And the Wright brothers
were born during the Civil War.

942
01:11:23,012 --> 01:11:25,139
I thought you went to school with them.

943
01:11:26,558 --> 01:11:29,102
Come on, Indy. That was funny.

944
01:11:29,185 --> 01:11:30,019
"Funny"?

945
01:11:31,145 --> 01:11:34,941
I'm stranded in Tangier
with a broken tuk-tuk and two thieves.

946
01:11:35,024 --> 01:11:36,276
I'm wanted for murder.

947
01:11:36,359 --> 01:11:38,403
The Nazis have half the dial...

948
01:11:38,486 --> 01:11:39,904
...and your father's notebooks.

949
01:11:41,114 --> 01:11:42,282
I have a copy.

950
01:11:43,116 --> 01:11:44,617
Of the notebooks. I copied them.

951
01:11:44,701 --> 01:11:45,577
Where?

952
01:11:46,661 --> 01:11:48,705
You memorized five notebooks?

953
01:11:48,788 --> 01:11:50,081
Seven. And of course not.

954
01:11:50,164 --> 01:11:51,791
Half of them were incredibly boring.

955
01:11:52,667 --> 01:11:55,086
Only the important parts.

956
01:11:56,087 --> 01:11:56,963
Like what?

957
01:11:57,422 --> 01:11:58,631
Where the Grafikos is.

958
01:11:58,715 --> 01:12:00,508
Nobody knows.

959
01:12:00,592 --> 01:12:02,010
- Dad did.
- He didn't know.

960
01:12:02,093 --> 01:12:03,344
- Yes, he did.
- No.

961
01:12:03,428 --> 01:12:05,138
- He knew.
- What's the Grafikos?

962
01:12:05,221 --> 01:12:06,598
Directions to the rest of the dial.

963
01:12:06,681 --> 01:12:08,349
Ask her if she knows what language it's in.

964
01:12:08,433 --> 01:12:10,351
None. It's in code.

965
01:12:10,435 --> 01:12:11,728
What code?

966
01:12:11,811 --> 01:12:13,354
Archimedes used two codes.

967
01:12:13,438 --> 01:12:15,064
Linear B and the Polybius square.

968
01:12:15,148 --> 01:12:18,526
Even if you find the Grafikos,
you can't read it without me.

969
01:12:19,027 --> 01:12:20,403
Please.

970
01:12:20,486 --> 01:12:22,405
Dad taught me Polybius when I was nine.

971
01:12:22,488 --> 01:12:24,157
He used to leave notes around the house.

972
01:12:24,240 --> 01:12:25,241
"Clean your room."

973
01:12:25,325 --> 01:12:26,618
"Stay away from my brandy."

974
01:12:27,744 --> 01:12:28,786
What if it's Linear B?

975
01:12:29,746 --> 01:12:31,122
Fifty pounds says it's Polybius.

976
01:12:32,165 --> 01:12:33,374
Give me that.

977
01:12:33,458 --> 01:12:34,375
Hey.

978
01:12:34,792 --> 01:12:36,085
That won't work.

979
01:12:36,169 --> 01:12:38,504
Moroccan chewing gum
is made from Manilkara sap.

980
01:12:38,588 --> 01:12:39,881
It's heat-resistant.

981
01:12:41,382 --> 01:12:42,592
Try to pull it off.

982
01:12:47,513 --> 01:12:48,681
- Pull it off.
- I'm trying.

983
01:12:50,767 --> 01:12:52,352
Huh?

984
01:12:53,061 --> 01:12:54,729
- Huh?
- It won't hold.

985
01:12:54,812 --> 01:12:56,814
It'll get us to the train station.

986
01:12:56,898 --> 01:12:58,107
"Us"?

987
01:12:58,191 --> 01:12:59,484
- Are you going home?
- No.

988
01:12:59,567 --> 01:13:01,444
To Casablanca.

989
01:13:01,528 --> 01:13:03,404
Then I'll fly to the Aegean.

990
01:13:03,488 --> 01:13:04,364
Like you.

991
01:13:04,781 --> 01:13:06,407
Why do you think we're going to the Aegean?

992
01:13:06,491 --> 01:13:09,661
You have to get to the Grafikos
before the Nazis.

993
01:13:09,744 --> 01:13:11,829
Archimedes was surrounded by Romans.

994
01:13:12,539 --> 01:13:13,831
Where else would it be?

995
01:13:13,915 --> 01:13:14,999
The Aegean is enormous.

996
01:13:15,083 --> 01:13:16,292
And you don't have coordinates.

997
01:13:16,376 --> 01:13:17,919
And you don't have a boat.

998
01:13:20,838 --> 01:13:23,341
I have an old friend in Greece.

999
01:13:23,424 --> 01:13:24,884
An expert diver.

1000
01:13:24,968 --> 01:13:26,886
With a big, beautiful boat...

1001
01:13:26,970 --> 01:13:29,013
...that will get us there before them.

1002
01:13:29,889 --> 01:13:32,016
You need me...

1003
01:13:32,100 --> 01:13:33,309
...and you know it.

1004
01:13:46,823 --> 01:13:48,074
TANGIER

1005
01:13:51,953 --> 01:13:53,496
CASABLANCA

1006
01:14:00,879 --> 01:14:03,131
GREECE

1007
01:14:03,214 --> 01:14:06,885
ATHENS

1008
01:14:23,443 --> 01:14:24,694
Renny!

1009
01:14:26,779 --> 01:14:27,655
Indy?

1010
01:14:28,656 --> 01:14:29,699
Indy!

1011
01:14:35,163 --> 01:14:37,207
That's the expert diver?

1012
01:14:37,290 --> 01:14:39,500
He's the best frogman in Spain.

1013
01:14:42,462 --> 01:14:43,379
Hello!

1014
01:14:43,463 --> 01:14:47,008
The best diver has a lousy boat
and only one good flipper?

1015
01:14:47,091 --> 01:14:48,134
Enough.

1016
01:14:52,013 --> 01:14:53,348
You've got another boat?

1017
01:14:53,431 --> 01:14:54,265
Enough.

1018
01:15:08,696 --> 01:15:09,739
We're heading here.

1019
01:15:11,366 --> 01:15:13,826
Where they found
the first part of the Antikythera.

1020
01:15:14,744 --> 01:15:15,703
But...

1021
01:15:17,247 --> 01:15:18,540
...we're going deeper.

1022
01:15:20,124 --> 01:15:23,920
Dad tracked down the old sponge diver
who found the dial.

1023
01:15:24,003 --> 01:15:25,255
He told him...

1024
01:15:25,338 --> 01:15:28,383
...the Roman shipwreck
had broken apart...

1025
01:15:28,466 --> 01:15:29,676
...twenty meters below the surface.

1026
01:15:29,759 --> 01:15:32,720
It held the skeletons
of more than a hundred centurions.

1027
01:15:33,888 --> 01:15:35,056
They found it there.

1028
01:15:36,683 --> 01:15:39,811
But he said most of the ship
had broken away...

1029
01:15:39,894 --> 01:15:41,646
...and sunk to the bottom of the ocean.

1030
01:15:42,605 --> 01:15:44,190
Too deep for sponge divers.

1031
01:15:45,233 --> 01:15:48,152
And Dad wondered
why a Roman warship...

1032
01:15:48,236 --> 01:15:51,614
...would sail from Syracuse with a hundred centurions.

1033
01:15:55,326 --> 01:15:58,705
They weren't taking half a dial on a pleasure cruise.

1034
01:16:02,542 --> 01:16:04,127
They had the Grafikos...

1035
01:16:07,005 --> 01:16:09,173
...and were looking for the rest of the dial.

1036
01:16:12,468 --> 01:16:14,095
Your father was a genius.

1037
01:16:14,721 --> 01:16:15,638
Yes.

1038
01:16:17,181 --> 01:16:18,141
He was.

1039
01:16:38,077 --> 01:16:39,078
Seven of spades.

1040
01:16:42,373 --> 01:16:43,541
How do you do that?

1041
01:16:43,625 --> 01:16:44,667
Wow.

1042
01:16:45,501 --> 01:16:46,336
Again.

1043
01:16:50,340 --> 01:16:52,217
Pick a card, Dr. Jones.

1044
01:16:59,724 --> 01:17:00,850
Seven of spades!

1045
01:17:02,977 --> 01:17:04,103
He does magic.

1046
01:17:04,187 --> 01:17:05,438
It's a trick deck.

1047
01:17:05,522 --> 01:17:06,481
No.

1048
01:17:07,690 --> 01:17:09,192
I force the card.

1049
01:17:09,275 --> 01:17:10,652
I give the target...

1050
01:17:10,735 --> 01:17:11,986
...you...

1051
01:17:12,070 --> 01:17:13,154
...the feeling of having a choice...

1052
01:17:13,238 --> 01:17:15,448
...while making them choose the card I want.

1053
01:17:17,575 --> 01:17:18,743
"The target."

1054
01:17:21,788 --> 01:17:23,790
Yes. Tomorrow's a big day.

1055
01:17:23,873 --> 01:17:25,667
I'll check the oxygen lines.

1056
01:17:31,673 --> 01:17:32,757
Good night.

1057
01:17:35,885 --> 01:17:39,013
Do you remember seeing dates
in your father's notebooks?

1058
01:17:40,348 --> 01:17:41,808
Dates?

1059
01:17:41,891 --> 01:17:43,184
What dates?

1060
01:17:44,102 --> 01:17:45,103
These.

1061
01:17:48,815 --> 01:17:51,276
Written over and over in this letter.

1062
01:17:52,694 --> 01:17:54,612
August 20, 1969.

1063
01:17:54,696 --> 01:17:56,322
That's in three days.

1064
01:17:56,406 --> 01:17:58,741
And the same date in 1939.

1065
01:17:58,825 --> 01:18:01,244
Two weeks before
Hitler invaded Poland.

1066
01:18:03,705 --> 01:18:04,664
Wait.

1067
01:18:05,456 --> 01:18:07,792
Now you think the dial
has magical powers?

1068
01:18:09,085 --> 01:18:11,129
I don't believe in magic, Wombat.

1069
01:18:13,006 --> 01:18:15,383
But sometimes in my life, I've seen things.

1070
01:18:18,511 --> 01:18:20,263
Things I can't explain.

1071
01:18:21,598 --> 01:18:24,058
And I've come to believe it isn't so much...

1072
01:18:24,142 --> 01:18:26,352
...what you believe.

1073
01:18:26,436 --> 01:18:28,688
But how strongly you believe it.

1074
01:18:31,816 --> 01:18:33,151
I've seen things too, Indy.

1075
01:18:34,068 --> 01:18:36,696
The only thing worth believing in...

1076
01:18:36,779 --> 01:18:38,448
...is money.

1077
01:18:40,742 --> 01:18:41,826
Sure.

1078
01:18:47,624 --> 01:18:48,750
What if you could go into the past?

1079
01:18:51,085 --> 01:18:52,128
What would you do?

1080
01:18:53,504 --> 01:18:54,923
See the Trojan War?

1081
01:18:56,216 --> 01:18:57,550
Visit Cleopatra?

1082
01:19:01,179 --> 01:19:03,306
I'd stop my son from enlisting.

1083
01:19:06,476 --> 01:19:08,186
Did he enlist to please you?

1084
01:19:09,270 --> 01:19:11,481
No, to spite me.

1085
01:19:15,151 --> 01:19:17,362
How would you have stopped him?

1086
01:19:21,533 --> 01:19:23,368
I'd have told him he was going to die.

1087
01:19:30,416 --> 01:19:32,293
I'd tell him his mother...

1088
01:19:34,921 --> 01:19:36,756
...would never find an end to her grief.

1089
01:19:38,591 --> 01:19:40,343
That his father...

1090
01:19:43,179 --> 01:19:45,098
...wouldn't be able to comfort her.

1091
01:19:48,393 --> 01:19:50,895
And that the loss would
destroy their marriage.

1092
01:20:04,909 --> 01:20:06,119
You still wear the ring.

1093
01:20:29,517 --> 01:20:30,476
That's promising.

1094
01:20:38,943 --> 01:20:40,361
Even more promising.

1095
01:20:46,159 --> 01:20:47,243
Hello.

1096
01:20:49,454 --> 01:20:51,456
Get your gear on. They're waiting.

1097
01:20:56,085 --> 01:20:56,920
Hello.

1098
01:20:58,171 --> 01:20:59,172
Come here.

1099
01:21:00,006 --> 01:21:01,507
All right.

1100
01:21:01,591 --> 01:21:02,467
I'll show you something.

1101
01:21:02,550 --> 01:21:04,469
God, this looks important.

1102
01:21:07,764 --> 01:21:08,681
Around the rope...

1103
01:21:08,765 --> 01:21:11,017
We're going down deep.

1104
01:21:11,100 --> 01:21:14,020
Descend quickly
and breathe through these hoses.

1105
01:21:14,103 --> 01:21:14,938
Okay?

1106
01:21:15,396 --> 01:21:17,023
Stay apart.

1107
01:21:17,106 --> 01:21:19,150
Don't get tangled. All right?

1108
01:21:19,234 --> 01:21:20,485
We can't stay long.

1109
01:21:20,568 --> 01:21:23,905
I have a method that usually
prevents decompression sickness.

1110
01:21:24,906 --> 01:21:26,032
- Usually?
- Yes.

1111
01:21:26,115 --> 01:21:27,408
I call it "the bounce."

1112
01:21:27,492 --> 01:21:28,576
Go down quickly...

1113
01:21:28,660 --> 01:21:30,662
...three minutes on the bottom, then back up.

1114
01:21:30,745 --> 01:21:31,871
Three minutes!

1115
01:21:31,955 --> 01:21:33,790
Not a second longer.

1116
01:21:34,457 --> 01:21:35,333
Three minutes.

1117
01:21:35,792 --> 01:21:36,793
Yes, that's it.

1118
01:21:36,876 --> 01:21:38,711
- What about my lighter?
- I don't know, Captain.

1119
01:21:39,921 --> 01:21:40,880
We have to go.

1120
01:21:43,716 --> 01:21:44,759
One quick question.

1121
01:21:44,842 --> 01:21:46,261
What's that?

1122
01:21:46,344 --> 01:21:47,720
- Sharks?
- No.

1123
01:21:47,804 --> 01:21:50,181
No sharks. They're eels.

1124
01:21:51,266 --> 01:21:52,183
Eels?

1125
01:21:53,726 --> 01:21:54,727
They're usually small.

1126
01:21:54,811 --> 01:21:56,479
Sometimes they're big. Two meters long.

1127
01:21:56,563 --> 01:21:58,690
But if you see them, don't move.

1128
01:21:58,773 --> 01:22:02,068
When they bite, their jaws lock. Yes.

1129
01:22:02,151 --> 01:22:03,570
They look like snakes.

1130
01:22:04,445 --> 01:22:05,321
They're not.

1131
01:22:05,864 --> 01:22:07,532
Private Brandy reporting for duty.

1132
01:22:08,157 --> 01:22:09,033
Aren't you coming, Teddy?

1133
01:22:09,784 --> 01:22:10,994
I can't swim.

1134
01:22:11,077 --> 01:22:12,287
Lucky him.

1135
01:22:12,370 --> 01:22:13,663
Everyone can swim.

1136
01:22:15,540 --> 01:22:17,500
Reach and pull.

1137
01:22:19,544 --> 01:22:21,796
Stay with Héctor
and watch our lines.

1138
01:22:25,633 --> 01:22:26,843
It's time, mate.

1139
01:24:15,285 --> 01:24:16,202
Two minutes?

1140
01:25:47,669 --> 01:25:48,503
I'll handle it.

1141
01:26:29,502 --> 01:26:30,336
Look.

1142
01:27:02,410 --> 01:27:03,244
Hurry!

1143
01:27:18,384 --> 01:27:19,886
Helena!

1144
01:27:28,811 --> 01:27:29,729
Come on.

1145
01:27:29,812 --> 01:27:31,356
That's it.

1146
01:27:32,190 --> 01:27:33,399
There we go.

1147
01:27:56,548 --> 01:27:57,799
Who are these people?

1148
01:28:00,260 --> 01:28:01,177
They're Nazis.

1149
01:28:01,261 --> 01:28:03,805
First you return my dial, Dr. Jones...

1150
01:28:03,888 --> 01:28:07,016
...and now you bring me the Grafikos.

1151
01:28:07,934 --> 01:28:10,103
I'm glad you're making good use
of your retirement.

1152
01:28:10,478 --> 01:28:12,939
I should have retired you when I had the chance.

1153
01:28:13,022 --> 01:28:14,232
And your sponsors?

1154
01:28:14,315 --> 01:28:15,984
I'm on my own now.

1155
01:28:16,067 --> 01:28:17,861
Things move forward, Dr. Jones.

1156
01:28:17,944 --> 01:28:18,820
And sometimes...

1157
01:28:20,697 --> 01:28:22,490
...backward.

1158
01:28:25,743 --> 01:28:27,996
Let's get comfortable and have a little chat.

1159
01:28:55,064 --> 01:28:56,524
What language is it?

1160
01:28:56,608 --> 01:28:58,193
It's not a language.

1161
01:28:59,444 --> 01:29:00,528
It's a code.

1162
01:29:01,487 --> 01:29:02,363
Polybius.

1163
01:29:03,198 --> 01:29:04,490
You owe me fifty pounds.

1164
01:29:06,826 --> 01:29:08,703
You don't know the Polybius cipher?

1165
01:29:08,786 --> 01:29:09,704
No.

1166
01:29:10,538 --> 01:29:11,956
But apparently you do.

1167
01:29:16,336 --> 01:29:17,295
Read it.

1168
01:29:25,720 --> 01:29:26,679
No.

1169
01:29:32,435 --> 01:29:33,436
Indy...

1170
01:29:44,531 --> 01:29:45,823
Feeling useful now?

1171
01:29:49,244 --> 01:29:50,078
I am.

1172
01:29:53,248 --> 01:29:54,582
I understand it perfectly.

1173
01:29:54,666 --> 01:29:55,542
No!

1174
01:29:57,418 --> 01:29:58,336
Sorry, Indy.

1175
01:29:58,753 --> 01:30:00,129
Dying nobly isn't my thing.

1176
01:30:00,797 --> 01:30:01,965
It's just...

1177
01:30:02,048 --> 01:30:03,466
...cold, hard mathematics.

1178
01:30:04,008 --> 01:30:05,843
I'll decipher it
and help you find the other half.

1179
01:30:07,262 --> 01:30:09,013
One hundred thousand dollars. Cash.

1180
01:30:09,097 --> 01:30:10,056
Helena...

1181
01:30:10,723 --> 01:30:11,683
Take it or leave it.

1182
01:30:13,226 --> 01:30:14,269
Deal.

1183
01:30:27,866 --> 01:30:29,576
He'll never pay you.

1184
01:30:31,119 --> 01:30:33,997
You'll find this is worth more than you asked.

1185
01:30:43,506 --> 01:30:45,091
- Teddy.
- Helena...

1186
01:30:45,174 --> 01:30:46,342
...don't do it.

1187
01:30:51,764 --> 01:30:53,182
Let's see what we've got here.

1188
01:30:55,351 --> 01:30:56,728
"My Makhana..."

1189
01:30:57,395 --> 01:30:58,229
...meaning "machine"...

1190
01:30:59,480 --> 01:31:03,109
"...lies with me in the city I left.

1191
01:31:03,193 --> 01:31:05,653
"Where wolves teach
men how to walk.

1192
01:31:05,737 --> 01:31:09,866
"Beneath one of nine, I lie."

1193
01:31:12,702 --> 01:31:14,495
Only one thing lies anywhere forever.

1194
01:31:14,579 --> 01:31:17,290
The dead.
If the dial lies with him, then...

1195
01:31:19,167 --> 01:31:20,668
...it's directions to his tomb.

1196
01:31:21,169 --> 01:31:22,503
Archimedes' tomb?

1197
01:31:23,922 --> 01:31:25,715
Undiscovered for two thousand years.

1198
01:31:28,801 --> 01:31:30,887
"The city I left."

1199
01:31:31,512 --> 01:31:34,974
Archimedes lived in two places,
so he only left one.

1200
01:31:35,850 --> 01:31:37,852
Anybody know? In the back?

1201
01:31:38,728 --> 01:31:40,355
Everyone knows, Indy.

1202
01:31:40,438 --> 01:31:41,356
Even this clown.

1203
01:31:41,439 --> 01:31:42,857
Alexandria.

1204
01:31:42,941 --> 01:31:43,983
Excellent.

1205
01:31:45,235 --> 01:31:47,487
The Greek word for "wolf" is "lykos."

1206
01:31:47,570 --> 01:31:49,906
It's the root of "lyceum," or "school."

1207
01:31:49,989 --> 01:31:52,617
And "to walk" in Doric is...

1208
01:31:54,035 --> 01:31:54,869
..."peripatio."

1209
01:31:56,162 --> 01:31:58,915
But it's also the word for...

1210
01:32:04,170 --> 01:32:06,130
I thought you were clever.

1211
01:32:08,716 --> 01:32:11,928
"Peripatio" means "to walk"...

1212
01:32:12,011 --> 01:32:14,514
...but also "to count."

1213
01:32:14,597 --> 01:32:17,725
"Where wolves teach
men to walk"...

1214
01:32:18,476 --> 01:32:20,770
- ...would be...
- The School of Mathematics.

1215
01:32:22,105 --> 01:32:23,773
Correct.

1216
01:32:23,857 --> 01:32:25,400
The next part is easy.

1217
01:32:26,693 --> 01:32:28,278
"Under one of nine"?

1218
01:32:29,445 --> 01:32:30,864
There are nine Muses.

1219
01:32:30,947 --> 01:32:32,156
The Mouseion.

1220
01:32:32,240 --> 01:32:34,784
In Greek,
the Great Library of Alexandria.

1221
01:32:34,868 --> 01:32:38,746
Which, as we know, has
nine statues holding up the roof.

1222
01:32:38,830 --> 01:32:39,747
May I?

1223
01:32:40,999 --> 01:32:41,833
Thank you.

1224
01:32:43,293 --> 01:32:46,045
The nine Muses, in reverse order...

1225
01:32:47,505 --> 01:32:49,007
...are Calliope...

1226
01:32:49,924 --> 01:32:50,758
...Urania...

1227
01:32:52,343 --> 01:32:53,386
...Polyhymnia...

1228
01:32:54,345 --> 01:32:55,889
- Erato...
- Polyhymnia.

1229
01:32:56,097 --> 01:32:58,474
...and Mel... whatever her name was...

1230
01:32:59,017 --> 01:32:59,851
...that other one...

1231
01:33:00,727 --> 01:33:01,603
...Thalia...

1232
01:33:02,353 --> 01:33:03,646
...Euterpe...

1233
01:33:03,730 --> 01:33:05,815
...but the first is...

1234
01:33:06,232 --> 01:33:07,066
...Clio.

1235
01:33:08,902 --> 01:33:10,153
Muse of history and time.

1236
01:33:10,236 --> 01:33:11,529
That Archimedes...

1237
01:33:11,613 --> 01:33:12,864
- ...was very clever.
- The entrance...

1238
01:33:12,947 --> 01:33:15,158
...to the tomb is
next to the School of Mathematics...

1239
01:33:15,241 --> 01:33:17,035
...beneath a statue of Clio...

1240
01:33:17,118 --> 01:33:19,954
...in the ruins
of the Great Library of Alexandria.

1241
01:33:20,038 --> 01:33:21,331
A gold star for Dr. Brainiac.

1242
01:33:22,874 --> 01:33:23,708
Teddy!

1243
01:33:33,760 --> 01:33:35,220
Go for the Grafikos!

1244
01:34:02,872 --> 01:34:05,667
I've said it before and I'll say it again!

1245
01:34:05,750 --> 01:34:08,378
When you're in trouble, dynamite.

1246
01:34:10,129 --> 01:34:11,673
You just killed my friend.

1247
01:34:17,178 --> 01:34:18,263
I'm sorry.

1248
01:34:18,346 --> 01:34:20,098
You told them everything.

1249
01:34:21,808 --> 01:34:22,892
Word for word.

1250
01:34:23,518 --> 01:34:25,436
But Archimedes didn't make it that easy.

1251
01:34:26,229 --> 01:34:28,147
The tomb isn't in Alexandria.

1252
01:34:41,703 --> 01:34:42,662
What do you see?

1253
01:34:43,329 --> 01:34:45,123
The Polybius square.

1254
01:34:45,665 --> 01:34:46,791
What's it made of?

1255
01:34:49,335 --> 01:34:50,545
Wax and wood.

1256
01:34:52,171 --> 01:34:53,423
Anything else?

1257
01:34:57,010 --> 01:34:57,886
It's heavy.

1258
01:35:00,930 --> 01:35:02,056
Too heavy.

1259
01:35:04,183 --> 01:35:05,310
Give me that.

1260
01:35:45,516 --> 01:35:47,310
Is it solid gold?

1261
01:35:47,393 --> 01:35:48,603
Ancient gold.

1262
01:35:50,104 --> 01:35:51,105
From the Nile.

1263
01:35:52,815 --> 01:35:53,775
Hold this, Teddy.

1264
01:36:05,286 --> 01:36:06,454
Sell this...

1265
01:36:06,538 --> 01:36:08,289
...and we'll never have money problems again.

1266
01:36:08,373 --> 01:36:09,374
I've got just the right buyer.

1267
01:36:09,457 --> 01:36:12,460
- An old duchess in Gibraltar...
- It's going to a museum.

1268
01:36:15,755 --> 01:36:17,048
Take the wheel, Teddy.

1269
01:36:23,930 --> 01:36:24,931
What does it say?

1270
01:36:27,058 --> 01:36:29,936
"Seek where Dionysius...

1271
01:36:30,728 --> 01:36:33,481
"...hears every whisper...

1272
01:36:34,315 --> 01:36:36,359
"...like a hurricane."

1273
01:36:39,195 --> 01:36:40,613
The Ear.

1274
01:36:41,322 --> 01:36:43,116
The Ear of Dionysius.

1275
01:36:43,992 --> 01:36:44,951
Where is it?

1276
01:36:46,411 --> 01:36:47,453
In Sicily.

1277
01:36:48,955 --> 01:36:51,040
- How much fuel have we got?
- Full tank.

1278
01:37:00,049 --> 01:37:01,134
They're heading west.

1279
01:37:01,217 --> 01:37:02,343
Syracuse.

1280
01:37:02,427 --> 01:37:03,428
Not east.

1281
01:37:03,511 --> 01:37:05,972
SICILY

1282
01:37:32,373 --> 01:37:33,374
I want the papaya.

1283
01:37:33,458 --> 01:37:34,500
How much is it?

1284
01:37:37,962 --> 01:37:38,838
Here.

1285
01:37:41,716 --> 01:37:43,885
Look at the boy with the straw hat.

1286
01:37:45,053 --> 01:37:45,970
This way.

1287
01:37:49,140 --> 01:37:49,974
Hey.

1288
01:37:51,601 --> 01:37:52,810
What do you think?

1289
01:37:59,859 --> 01:38:01,110
What are we doing?

1290
01:38:02,737 --> 01:38:04,489
Waiting for everything to close at the caves.

1291
01:38:04,572 --> 01:38:06,157
It's packed with tourists.

1292
01:38:06,241 --> 01:38:08,660
Is he in charge now?

1293
01:38:10,036 --> 01:38:11,454
No, Teddy.

1294
01:38:12,830 --> 01:38:15,959
I thought we were in this
for the wrong reasons.

1295
01:38:16,543 --> 01:38:17,710
We are.

1296
01:38:21,214 --> 01:38:22,173
No question about it.

1297
01:38:22,257 --> 01:38:25,051
You know he'll never
let us sell any of this.

1298
01:38:26,553 --> 01:38:28,304
I'm still in charge, Teddy.

1299
01:38:28,388 --> 01:38:30,390
Helena, come here! Help me!

1300
01:38:36,312 --> 01:38:37,438
What a waste.

1301
01:38:38,773 --> 01:38:39,816
Here's your backpack.

1302
01:38:39,899 --> 01:38:40,859
Where's the boy?

1303
01:38:54,873 --> 01:38:55,915
Watch out!

1304
01:38:56,541 --> 01:38:57,792
What's wrong with you?

1305
01:39:26,321 --> 01:39:27,488
Hello.

1306
01:39:29,073 --> 01:39:29,908
Hello.

1307
01:39:33,036 --> 01:39:33,912
Let me go!

1308
01:39:35,205 --> 01:39:36,247
Let me go!

1309
01:39:44,047 --> 01:39:46,257
Let me go!

1310
01:39:49,928 --> 01:39:50,803
Hey!

1311
01:40:01,981 --> 01:40:03,066
Helena!

1312
01:40:04,442 --> 01:40:05,902
They've got Teddy.

1313
01:40:07,987 --> 01:40:09,572
They took him up the hill.

1314
01:40:29,676 --> 01:40:30,843
What are they going to do to him?

1315
01:40:30,927 --> 01:40:33,596
He knows about the Ear,
what the Grafikos says.

1316
01:40:34,973 --> 01:40:36,516
They won't hurt him.

1317
01:40:36,599 --> 01:40:37,767
They'll use him...

1318
01:40:38,518 --> 01:40:40,853
...to find the other half of the dial.

1319
01:40:40,937 --> 01:40:42,313
We have to get there first.

1320
01:40:55,076 --> 01:40:59,205
The colonizing queens
built many cities in Sicily.

1321
01:40:59,289 --> 01:41:00,790
- This...
- Keep moving.

1322
01:41:01,457 --> 01:41:04,002
...is the oldest Greek temple.

1323
01:41:05,086 --> 01:41:06,462
The cave is over there.

1324
01:41:16,848 --> 01:41:18,600
Can we move a little faster?

1325
01:41:27,817 --> 01:41:29,277
The Ear of Dionysius.

1326
01:41:36,993 --> 01:41:37,952
Wow.

1327
01:41:52,217 --> 01:41:55,720
"Seek where Dionysius hears
every whisper like a hurricane."

1328
01:41:56,930 --> 01:41:57,931
What an echo.

1329
01:41:59,682 --> 01:42:02,268
Make noise until you find where it's loudest.

1330
01:42:19,702 --> 01:42:20,537
Stop.

1331
01:42:28,461 --> 01:42:29,587
It's here.

1332
01:42:34,551 --> 01:42:35,552
A Greek pediment.

1333
01:42:36,719 --> 01:42:37,720
An entrance.

1334
01:42:39,138 --> 01:42:40,139
It used to be.

1335
01:42:43,434 --> 01:42:44,435
A crescent moon.

1336
01:42:47,313 --> 01:42:48,398
Like the one on the Grafikos.

1337
01:42:55,989 --> 01:42:57,824
There's an opening up there.

1338
01:43:04,581 --> 01:43:05,456
Are you all right up there?

1339
01:43:06,624 --> 01:43:07,458
Yes.

1340
01:43:10,295 --> 01:43:11,337
You're not moving.

1341
01:43:13,840 --> 01:43:15,049
I'm thinking.

1342
01:43:18,011 --> 01:43:18,887
About what?

1343
01:43:20,054 --> 01:43:22,974
What the hell I'm doing up here,
forty feet in the air...

1344
01:43:23,308 --> 01:43:24,976
...with bad shoulders...

1345
01:43:25,059 --> 01:43:26,936
...a bad spine...

1346
01:43:27,020 --> 01:43:29,606
...a plate in one leg
and screws in the other.

1347
01:43:29,689 --> 01:43:30,940
Yes, I understand.

1348
01:43:31,566 --> 01:43:32,692
No, you don't.

1349
01:43:33,526 --> 01:43:34,736
You're half my age.

1350
01:43:35,778 --> 01:43:38,531
You never had to drink the blood of Kali.

1351
01:43:38,615 --> 01:43:39,866
No. Fair point.

1352
01:43:40,700 --> 01:43:42,452
Or get tortured with voodoo.

1353
01:43:43,828 --> 01:43:46,664
And I'm guessing you haven't
been shot nine times...

1354
01:43:46,748 --> 01:43:49,209
...including the time your father shot me.

1355
01:43:50,960 --> 01:43:52,295
Dad shot you?

1356
01:43:53,004 --> 01:43:54,255
Don't talk to me.

1357
01:43:54,339 --> 01:43:55,506
Keep going. I'll follow you.

1358
01:44:04,265 --> 01:44:05,225
No.

1359
01:44:05,308 --> 01:44:06,935
We're closed.

1360
01:44:07,018 --> 01:44:08,102
Look around you.

1361
01:44:08,186 --> 01:44:09,771
- We're closed.
- The Ear of Dionysius.

1362
01:44:09,854 --> 01:44:10,730
No.

1363
01:44:10,813 --> 01:44:12,732
It's closed for renovations.

1364
01:44:14,817 --> 01:44:15,860
I'll ask one more time.

1365
01:44:15,944 --> 01:44:17,862
Where is the Ear of Dionysius?

1366
01:44:37,507 --> 01:44:39,676
What's wrong? Worried about the boy?

1367
01:44:40,969 --> 01:44:42,720
Are you sure they won't hurt him?

1368
01:44:42,804 --> 01:44:44,097
He'll be fine.

1369
01:44:44,180 --> 01:44:45,223
He's smart.

1370
01:44:47,100 --> 01:44:48,476
Where did you find him?

1371
01:44:50,186 --> 01:44:54,941
He tried to steal my purse outside
a casino in Marrakesh when he was ten.

1372
01:44:55,024 --> 01:44:56,943
I hit him with my car door...

1373
01:44:57,026 --> 01:44:59,612
...but he wouldn't let go and neither would I.

1374
01:44:59,696 --> 01:45:01,239
We've been together ever since.

1375
01:45:02,365 --> 01:45:04,325
I thought money was all you cared about.

1376
01:45:05,451 --> 01:45:06,452
It is.

1377
01:45:09,539 --> 01:45:13,668
No one memorizes every page
of their dead father's notebooks...

1378
01:45:13,751 --> 01:45:14,836
...for money.

1379
01:45:21,467 --> 01:45:22,468
Herr Voller!

1380
01:45:33,104 --> 01:45:34,480
They went that way.

1381
01:45:44,824 --> 01:45:45,867
Why?

1382
01:45:49,787 --> 01:45:51,623
Come on.

1383
01:45:54,375 --> 01:45:55,293
Move.

1384
01:46:00,131 --> 01:46:01,049
Good heavens...

1385
01:46:04,052 --> 01:46:05,553
Oh, my God!

1386
01:46:06,387 --> 01:46:07,722
Oh, my God!

1387
01:46:07,931 --> 01:46:09,307
- God!
- No.

1388
01:46:10,225 --> 01:46:11,601
Oh, my God!

1389
01:46:16,147 --> 01:46:17,273
Are they off me?

1390
01:46:17,357 --> 01:46:18,274
- Get them off!
- No.

1391
01:46:18,358 --> 01:46:19,526
- Get them off!
- Wait.

1392
01:46:32,580 --> 01:46:33,540
What's that?

1393
01:46:36,626 --> 01:46:37,669
Methane.

1394
01:46:39,170 --> 01:46:40,463
Don't breathe.

1395
01:46:40,547 --> 01:46:41,548
Don't breathe?

1396
01:46:45,260 --> 01:46:46,636
We have to get out of here.

1397
01:46:55,562 --> 01:46:56,479
Athena.

1398
01:46:58,231 --> 01:46:59,524
Goddess of war.

1399
01:47:02,402 --> 01:47:03,611
And reason.

1400
01:47:07,907 --> 01:47:09,284
"Under the moon..."

1401
01:47:11,578 --> 01:47:13,580
"...life lies at her feet."

1402
01:47:21,337 --> 01:47:22,672
Water displacement.

1403
01:47:23,464 --> 01:47:24,883
Get into the pool!

1404
01:47:24,966 --> 01:47:27,051
Why? Help me open the door.

1405
01:47:27,135 --> 01:47:28,887
They didn't get out through the doors!

1406
01:47:28,970 --> 01:47:29,846
Come to the pool!

1407
01:47:30,889 --> 01:47:32,849
I'm getting into the pool.

1408
01:47:32,932 --> 01:47:34,058
Help me.

1409
01:47:36,769 --> 01:47:38,813
Archimedes was fascinated by...

1410
01:47:40,315 --> 01:47:42,275
...water displacement!

1411
01:48:23,483 --> 01:48:24,609
Hey! Come back!

1412
01:48:24,692 --> 01:48:25,568
Klaber!

1413
01:48:39,791 --> 01:48:40,959
You don't have to do that!

1414
01:49:07,151 --> 01:49:07,986
Hey!

1415
01:49:09,195 --> 01:49:10,029
Give me that!

1416
01:49:11,990 --> 01:49:12,824
No!

1417
01:49:20,248 --> 01:49:21,541
Leave them alone!

1418
01:49:22,250 --> 01:49:23,877
I said leave them alone.

1419
01:49:48,318 --> 01:49:49,944
Reach and pull.

1420
01:49:50,028 --> 01:49:51,696
Reach and pull.

1421
01:49:51,779 --> 01:49:53,948
Reach and pull.

1422
01:50:22,393 --> 01:50:23,937
Archimedes' tomb.

1423
01:50:52,882 --> 01:50:54,175
Grab that corner.

1424
01:51:40,513 --> 01:51:41,431
Indy.

1425
01:51:43,808 --> 01:51:45,184
This frieze...

1426
01:51:47,854 --> 01:51:49,189
It's a phoenix.

1427
01:51:49,272 --> 01:51:50,356
A common symbol.

1428
01:51:50,440 --> 01:51:52,025
No. Look, it isn't.

1429
01:51:53,610 --> 01:51:55,320
This phoenix has propellers.

1430
01:52:04,204 --> 01:52:05,663
What's this doing here?

1431
01:52:14,464 --> 01:52:17,467
Clocks won't be invented
for another thousand years...

1432
01:52:18,134 --> 01:52:19,594
...let alone wristwatches.

1433
01:52:22,138 --> 01:52:23,348
Archimedes wore it.

1434
01:52:26,893 --> 01:52:28,269
Dad was right.

1435
01:52:29,020 --> 01:52:29,938
It works.

1436
01:52:30,021 --> 01:52:31,648
Of course it does, Miss Shaw.

1437
01:52:34,359 --> 01:52:35,610
Mathematics works.

1438
01:52:36,736 --> 01:52:39,739
You conquered space
and you will conquer time.

1439
01:52:47,622 --> 01:52:49,624
You've already lost your son, Dr. Jones.

1440
01:52:49,707 --> 01:52:50,708
Your wife has left you.

1441
01:52:50,792 --> 01:52:52,710
Do you want to lose your goddaughter too?

1442
01:52:55,797 --> 01:52:56,881
Why?

1443
01:52:57,298 --> 01:52:59,467
For a world that doesn't care
about people like us?

1444
01:53:10,979 --> 01:53:11,813
Thank you.

1445
01:53:50,518 --> 01:53:51,895
The greatest moment in history.

1446
01:53:58,985 --> 01:54:00,278
Its end.

1447
01:54:08,036 --> 01:54:08,953
Throw me the gun!

1448
01:54:19,339 --> 01:54:20,673
Get him out of here.

1449
01:54:20,757 --> 01:54:21,591
Teddy.

1450
01:54:22,634 --> 01:54:23,843
Teddy!

1451
01:54:23,927 --> 01:54:25,094
Come on! Go!

1452
01:54:25,178 --> 01:54:26,054
This way. Quick.

1453
01:54:30,141 --> 01:54:30,975
Go!

1454
01:54:35,438 --> 01:54:36,272
Indy!

1455
01:54:38,274 --> 01:54:39,526
Go!

1456
01:54:39,609 --> 01:54:40,944
Go!

1457
01:54:41,027 --> 01:54:42,111
Stop!

1458
01:54:45,490 --> 01:54:46,908
What now?

1459
01:54:48,535 --> 01:54:49,536
Bring him.

1460
01:54:57,627 --> 01:54:58,711
Come on, Helena!

1461
01:54:58,795 --> 01:55:00,213
- We can't leave him.
- We won't!

1462
01:55:00,296 --> 01:55:01,506
Follow me!

1463
01:55:03,466 --> 01:55:04,467
Move!

1464
01:55:08,388 --> 01:55:09,222
Come on.

1465
01:55:23,653 --> 01:55:25,613
Teddy. Let's go. We've got a car.

1466
01:55:30,535 --> 01:55:31,494
Shit.

1467
01:55:37,709 --> 01:55:38,751
Stay here.

1468
01:55:57,645 --> 01:55:58,646
Get in.

1469
01:56:02,275 --> 01:56:05,195
The first hemisphere sets the destination.

1470
01:56:06,696 --> 01:56:10,283
The second calculates
the location of the fissure...

1471
01:56:11,534 --> 01:56:13,369
...in Alexandrian coordinates.

1472
01:56:13,953 --> 01:56:16,831
Have Messner convert them
into latitude and longitude...

1473
01:56:17,874 --> 01:56:19,751
...and relay the waypoints
to the pilots.

1474
01:56:31,888 --> 01:56:33,389
A shortcut.

1475
01:56:37,685 --> 01:56:42,065
- Got the coordinates for 37.07?
- Who is it?

1476
01:56:42,815 --> 01:56:44,067
Churchill?

1477
01:56:45,401 --> 01:56:46,402
Ike?

1478
01:56:48,738 --> 01:56:51,241
Who are you going to kill to win the war?

1479
01:56:52,075 --> 01:56:55,036
In a few minutes,
with Archimedes' help...

1480
01:56:55,119 --> 01:56:56,996
...we will fly into the eye of this storm...

1481
01:56:57,789 --> 01:57:00,124
...and cross Sicilian airspace...

1482
01:57:00,208 --> 01:57:03,711
...on August 20, 1939.

1483
01:57:04,462 --> 01:57:08,007
We'll head north through friendly skies
with enough fuel to reach Munich.

1484
01:57:09,300 --> 01:57:11,010
My target will be waiting for me...

1485
01:57:11,094 --> 01:57:13,930
...at 16 Prinzregentenplatz...

1486
01:57:14,305 --> 01:57:16,516
...so I can brief him on his V-1 rocket.

1487
01:57:23,523 --> 01:57:27,485
What kind of Nazi kills the Führer?

1488
01:57:29,112 --> 01:57:31,781
One who believes in victory, Dr. Jones.

1489
01:57:37,036 --> 01:57:40,957
Hitler lit a fire
that could have burned for a thousand years.

1490
01:57:41,040 --> 01:57:42,542
I saw every mistake.

1491
01:57:43,334 --> 01:57:44,711
Every blunder.

1492
01:57:44,794 --> 01:57:46,546
And I will correct them all.

1493
01:57:46,629 --> 01:57:49,257
History is
a long list of defeats, Dr. Jones.

1494
01:57:50,300 --> 01:57:51,551
It's a matter of who loses.

1495
01:58:04,230 --> 01:58:05,231
Stop!

1496
01:59:18,388 --> 01:59:19,222
Teddy.

1497
01:59:21,057 --> 01:59:22,058
Can you fly one of those?

1498
01:59:24,227 --> 01:59:25,061
A Nord?

1499
01:59:27,814 --> 01:59:28,773
Of course.

1500
01:59:29,524 --> 01:59:30,733
You don't sound very sure.

1501
01:59:30,817 --> 01:59:32,527
I've never flown a Nord.

1502
01:59:32,610 --> 01:59:34,153
You've never flown any plane.

1503
01:59:36,030 --> 01:59:37,240
I'll get it started.

1504
01:59:37,323 --> 01:59:38,575
No, Teddy.

1505
01:59:39,951 --> 01:59:40,785
Shit.

1506
02:00:08,146 --> 02:00:09,814
Fasten your seat belt, Dr. Jones.

1507
02:00:09,898 --> 02:00:11,232
There may be turbulence.

1508
02:00:12,192 --> 02:00:14,736
You're German, Voller.

1509
02:00:14,819 --> 02:00:16,821
Don't try to be funny.

1510
02:00:40,220 --> 02:00:42,055
Come on, loudmouth.

1511
02:00:57,028 --> 02:00:57,862
Good.

1512
02:01:14,712 --> 02:01:15,755
All right.

1513
02:01:16,464 --> 02:01:19,008
Gauges green. Full power.

1514
02:01:19,676 --> 02:01:22,178
And when I hit 85 miles an hour, I rotate.

1515
02:01:59,924 --> 02:02:01,342
Flaps down.

1516
02:02:03,428 --> 02:02:05,430
All right, 85 miles an hour.

1517
02:02:05,889 --> 02:02:06,848
Rotate.

1518
02:03:14,749 --> 02:03:16,084
Time to destination?

1519
02:03:17,293 --> 02:03:18,836
Sixty seconds.

1520
02:03:24,175 --> 02:03:25,635
Continental drift.

1521
02:03:29,055 --> 02:03:30,265
Continental drift!

1522
02:03:32,016 --> 02:03:34,602
Archimedes didn't know
about continental drift.

1523
02:03:35,436 --> 02:03:36,688
He couldn't have known!

1524
02:03:37,689 --> 02:03:39,607
It hadn't been observed yet.

1525
02:03:40,608 --> 02:03:42,402
It's off.

1526
02:03:42,861 --> 02:03:44,737
His coordinates are based on landmarks...

1527
02:03:45,488 --> 02:03:48,783
...that moved over two thousand years!

1528
02:03:48,867 --> 02:03:52,287
It could be off by ten degrees or...

1529
02:04:07,260 --> 02:04:08,845
Thirty seconds.

1530
02:04:10,096 --> 02:04:11,014
Herr Voller...

1531
02:04:11,097 --> 02:04:13,808
Your calculations are wrong!

1532
02:04:14,392 --> 02:04:15,560
- Herr Voller...
- Shut up.

1533
02:04:16,227 --> 02:04:17,228
I'm thinking!

1534
02:04:20,190 --> 02:04:21,107
Twenty seconds.

1535
02:04:23,902 --> 02:04:24,986
Hold your course!

1536
02:04:26,988 --> 02:04:28,281
Fifteen seconds.

1537
02:04:31,826 --> 02:04:33,369
I don't know where we're going, Jürgen.

1538
02:04:33,453 --> 02:04:34,287
Ten seconds.

1539
02:04:34,913 --> 02:04:35,914
Nine!

1540
02:04:35,997 --> 02:04:36,873
But I'm sure...

1541
02:04:36,956 --> 02:04:37,790
Eight!

1542
02:04:37,874 --> 02:04:38,708
Seven!

1543
02:04:38,791 --> 02:04:39,626
...it's not 1939!

1544
02:04:39,709 --> 02:04:40,543
Six!

1545
02:04:40,627 --> 02:04:41,753
Turn around!

1546
02:04:42,462 --> 02:04:43,338
Abort!

1547
02:04:43,838 --> 02:04:44,672
Turn around!

1548
02:04:45,548 --> 02:04:46,382
Abort!!!

1549
02:04:46,674 --> 02:04:48,635
We're being pulled in!

1550
02:04:58,645 --> 02:04:59,979
What are you doing?

1551
02:05:00,188 --> 02:05:01,022
This is my plane!

1552
02:05:01,356 --> 02:05:02,690
You're flying my plane!

1553
02:05:27,507 --> 02:05:28,675
We're going to DIE!!!

1554
02:05:30,009 --> 02:05:31,719
We have to climb!

1555
02:05:53,575 --> 02:05:54,742
Restart the engines!

1556
02:06:23,438 --> 02:06:24,606
That's Sicily.

1557
02:06:24,689 --> 02:06:25,899
1939.

1558
02:06:27,650 --> 02:06:29,152
I did it.

1559
02:06:29,235 --> 02:06:30,820
I did it, Dr. Jones!

1560
02:06:30,904 --> 02:06:32,614
Entering the coordinates for Munich.

1561
02:06:39,579 --> 02:06:41,623
Yesterday belongs to us, Dr. Jones.

1562
02:07:06,523 --> 02:07:08,691
Those are Roman triremes.

1563
02:07:36,928 --> 02:07:38,137
Keep it in the air!

1564
02:07:58,324 --> 02:07:59,158
Master...

1565
02:07:59,242 --> 02:08:01,661
The Romans are approaching.

1566
02:08:03,413 --> 02:08:04,289
They have dragons!

1567
02:08:09,002 --> 02:08:10,378
Incredible.

1568
02:08:19,846 --> 02:08:20,805
Open the door!

1569
02:08:25,768 --> 02:08:26,769
What are they doing?

1570
02:08:27,729 --> 02:08:29,522
What are you doing, you idiots?

1571
02:08:29,606 --> 02:08:31,399
They think we're a monster!

1572
02:08:37,864 --> 02:08:39,449
You have to turn around.

1573
02:08:39,532 --> 02:08:41,451
It's the siege of Syracuse.

1574
02:08:42,577 --> 02:08:43,995
214 BC.

1575
02:08:44,078 --> 02:08:45,288
You picked the wrong war.

1576
02:08:49,042 --> 02:08:49,876
Master...

1577
02:08:53,296 --> 02:08:54,547
Don't go, Master!

1578
02:08:55,131 --> 02:08:56,341
The dragons!!

1579
02:09:06,601 --> 02:09:10,104
We must bring down their dragons!

1580
02:09:17,278 --> 02:09:18,196
Chief!

1581
02:09:18,279 --> 02:09:19,113
Change course!

1582
02:09:19,364 --> 02:09:20,740
The portal closes in minutes!

1583
02:09:21,157 --> 02:09:22,075
I have to get back!

1584
02:09:22,534 --> 02:09:23,660
I can't be here!

1585
02:09:23,868 --> 02:09:25,036
I CAN'T STAY HERE!

1586
02:09:25,119 --> 02:09:26,579
The plane won't survive!

1587
02:09:56,359 --> 02:09:57,277
Get out of my way!

1588
02:10:03,616 --> 02:10:05,743
How do you like that, savages?

1589
02:10:08,788 --> 02:10:11,541
Sorry, buddy, but you're a Nazi!

1590
02:10:21,593 --> 02:10:23,761
What are you doing here?

1591
02:10:23,845 --> 02:10:25,388
Rescuing you!

1592
02:10:36,941 --> 02:10:37,817
Get him out of here!

1593
02:10:39,194 --> 02:10:40,278
Indy!

1594
02:10:45,909 --> 02:10:47,118
Parachute!

1595
02:10:49,913 --> 02:10:51,080
I've got a parachute!

1596
02:10:52,582 --> 02:10:53,416
Hold on!

1597
02:10:57,212 --> 02:10:58,087
Let him go!

1598
02:11:00,423 --> 02:11:01,841
Give me the parachute!

1599
02:11:07,889 --> 02:11:10,016
Grab it. Hold on!

1600
02:11:12,477 --> 02:11:13,311
Indy!

1601
02:11:40,672 --> 02:11:42,131
We're losing altitude!

1602
02:11:43,383 --> 02:11:44,300
We're going down!

1603
02:11:46,219 --> 02:11:47,804
I've lost control!

1604
02:12:06,030 --> 02:12:08,575
Hey, those are my friends.

1605
02:12:08,658 --> 02:12:10,702
They're my friends. We have to help them!

1606
02:12:11,870 --> 02:12:13,454
They're my friends!

1607
02:13:15,350 --> 02:13:16,726
Eureka.

1608
02:13:33,910 --> 02:13:34,869
Try to land.

1609
02:13:34,953 --> 02:13:36,955
Come on, Indy. Help me.

1610
02:13:37,705 --> 02:13:39,832
Get up. Yes.

1611
02:13:39,916 --> 02:13:42,210
We have to get you out of here. Yes.

1612
02:13:44,128 --> 02:13:45,421
Help me. Sit down.

1613
02:13:46,297 --> 02:13:47,632
Sit down. You have to help me.

1614
02:13:47,715 --> 02:13:49,676
I know it hurts,
but we have to get you back.

1615
02:13:49,759 --> 02:13:52,470
This is incredible, Wombat.

1616
02:13:53,012 --> 02:13:54,973
Incredible.

1617
02:13:56,516 --> 02:13:58,309
Yes, it's wonderful.

1618
02:13:58,393 --> 02:13:59,978
But we have to get you out of here.

1619
02:14:02,105 --> 02:14:03,231
Oh, my God.

1620
02:14:04,899 --> 02:14:06,776
We're witnessing history.

1621
02:14:13,867 --> 02:14:15,743
Reverse these numbers.

1622
02:14:15,827 --> 02:14:16,911
They'll take you home.

1623
02:14:18,746 --> 02:14:19,581
What?

1624
02:14:22,500 --> 02:14:23,710
I'm staying.

1625
02:14:24,460 --> 02:14:26,462
No. You're not serious.

1626
02:14:29,299 --> 02:14:30,341
You are serious.

1627
02:14:34,429 --> 02:14:36,222
Indy, you've been shot.

1628
02:14:36,306 --> 02:14:37,140
You're bleeding.

1629
02:14:37,515 --> 02:14:39,183
You can't stay.

1630
02:14:39,267 --> 02:14:40,143
Yes, I can.

1631
02:14:40,810 --> 02:14:41,853
But why?

1632
02:14:41,936 --> 02:14:45,481
For a long, painful death
with poultices and leeches?

1633
02:14:46,524 --> 02:14:48,651
I've imagined this, Wombat.

1634
02:14:49,736 --> 02:14:51,654
I've studied it.

1635
02:14:51,738 --> 02:14:53,865
- All my life.
- Yes.

1636
02:14:53,948 --> 02:14:57,118
And if you stay here, you'll ruin everything.

1637
02:14:57,202 --> 02:14:58,036
And you'll die.

1638
02:14:58,786 --> 02:15:00,413
Get up, please.

1639
02:15:02,165 --> 02:15:03,333
Helena!

1640
02:15:03,875 --> 02:15:04,751
I did it!

1641
02:15:04,834 --> 02:15:07,253
Bravo, Teddy! Three cheers!

1642
02:15:07,337 --> 02:15:08,963
Indy, we have to go. Get up.

1643
02:15:09,047 --> 02:15:10,590
- I have to get you on the plane.
- No.

1644
02:15:10,673 --> 02:15:11,674
- We have to.
- No.

1645
02:15:11,758 --> 02:15:13,384
Get up. You can... We can!

1646
02:15:26,564 --> 02:15:27,398
Master!

1647
02:15:30,068 --> 02:15:31,861
We have to go, Master.

1648
02:15:33,488 --> 02:15:34,489
It's him.

1649
02:15:43,456 --> 02:15:45,500
He's asking how far we've traveled.

1650
02:15:48,920 --> 02:15:51,673
Two thousand years.

1651
02:15:52,298 --> 02:15:53,466
We've traveled...

1652
02:15:54,050 --> 02:15:57,053
...two thousand years.

1653
02:15:59,389 --> 02:16:02,517
But we never expected to meet...

1654
02:16:03,059 --> 02:16:05,562
...the great...

1655
02:16:06,104 --> 02:16:08,147
...Archimedes.

1656
02:16:13,820 --> 02:16:14,737
You...

1657
02:16:15,947 --> 02:16:17,282
...were always...

1658
02:16:17,949 --> 02:16:19,200
...going to...

1659
02:16:19,784 --> 02:16:21,369
...meet me.

1660
02:16:22,120 --> 02:16:23,997
That dial is a trick deck.

1661
02:16:24,080 --> 02:16:25,582
It only brings us here.

1662
02:16:25,665 --> 02:16:27,041
He made it to get help.

1663
02:16:27,125 --> 02:16:29,752
We've just terrified
the entire Roman navy...

1664
02:16:29,836 --> 02:16:30,753
...so we helped quite a bit.

1665
02:16:30,962 --> 02:16:32,588
I'm sorry, Archimedes.

1666
02:16:32,797 --> 02:16:34,132
I'm your biggest fan...

1667
02:16:34,382 --> 02:16:36,467
...but we have to go. He's injured.

1668
02:16:36,551 --> 02:16:37,510
Helena...

1669
02:16:37,594 --> 02:16:38,720
He can't keep that.

1670
02:16:38,802 --> 02:16:39,762
He has to make his own.

1671
02:16:39,846 --> 02:16:41,556
Helena, we have to leave!

1672
02:16:42,015 --> 02:16:44,225
Indy, we're running out of time!

1673
02:16:44,309 --> 02:16:45,435
We can't get stranded here!

1674
02:16:46,769 --> 02:16:47,686
I want...

1675
02:16:47,770 --> 02:16:48,855
...to stay...

1676
02:16:49,230 --> 02:16:51,065
...with you.

1677
02:16:51,149 --> 02:16:53,109
No! Don't say yes!

1678
02:16:53,693 --> 02:16:55,945
You are a brilliant man...

1679
02:16:56,195 --> 02:16:58,198
...a wise man...

1680
02:16:58,739 --> 02:17:00,158
...a genius...

1681
02:17:00,240 --> 02:17:01,159
Helena...

1682
02:17:01,242 --> 02:17:03,578
...a hero to your people.

1683
02:17:04,287 --> 02:17:07,832
But he can't help you.

1684
02:17:08,917 --> 02:17:10,627
This is your time.

1685
02:17:10,710 --> 02:17:11,586
This is your time.

1686
02:17:11,668 --> 02:17:13,712
He has to be in his own time.

1687
02:17:13,796 --> 02:17:14,963
He needs to be in his own.

1688
02:17:15,048 --> 02:17:16,007
His life isn't over.

1689
02:17:16,382 --> 02:17:17,425
He needs to go home.

1690
02:17:17,507 --> 02:17:19,177
At home there are medicines.

1691
02:17:19,260 --> 02:17:21,053
He can't die here. He can't.

1692
02:17:22,721 --> 02:17:25,558
Helena, get on the plane.

1693
02:17:28,144 --> 02:17:29,229
I'll be fine.

1694
02:17:31,064 --> 02:17:32,440
No, you won't.

1695
02:17:35,360 --> 02:17:37,111
I need to do this.

1696
02:17:38,363 --> 02:17:39,196
So do I.

1697
02:18:33,835 --> 02:18:34,711
Good morning.

1698
02:18:39,591 --> 02:18:40,967
How's the shoulder?

1699
02:18:43,636 --> 02:18:46,139
Better than my jaw.

1700
02:18:49,766 --> 02:18:50,767
Of course.

1701
02:18:52,770 --> 02:18:54,272
You should've let me stay.

1702
02:18:55,148 --> 02:18:56,356
I couldn't.

1703
02:19:05,365 --> 02:19:06,284
Why not?

1704
02:19:08,244 --> 02:19:10,788
For starters,
you'd have changed history.

1705
02:19:15,126 --> 02:19:16,919
Is that supposed to be a bad thing?

1706
02:19:21,049 --> 02:19:22,842
You're meant to be here, Indy.

1707
02:19:26,721 --> 02:19:27,555
Here.

1708
02:19:32,769 --> 02:19:33,895
For whom?

1709
02:20:01,214 --> 02:20:02,799
Marion.

1710
02:20:04,050 --> 02:20:05,093
Hello.

1711
02:20:24,445 --> 02:20:25,363
Teddy.

1712
02:20:26,990 --> 02:20:27,949
Marion.

1713
02:20:30,743 --> 02:20:31,703
What are you doing?

1714
02:20:33,246 --> 02:20:34,414
Putting away groceries.

1715
02:20:36,708 --> 02:20:39,419
There wasn't a scrap of food in here.

1716
02:20:40,336 --> 02:20:42,338
No. Really.

1717
02:20:47,135 --> 02:20:49,345
Someone told me you were back.

1718
02:20:56,811 --> 02:20:58,438
Are you back to your old self, Indy?

1719
02:21:00,064 --> 02:21:01,858
There were explosives...

1720
02:21:01,941 --> 02:21:04,152
Indy and Marion were running...

1721
02:21:04,235 --> 02:21:06,905
...and then suddenly the plane exploded.

1722
02:21:10,617 --> 02:21:12,660
Indy, you're on your feet.

1723
02:21:13,870 --> 02:21:15,246
Yes.

1724
02:21:16,539 --> 02:21:17,373
Yes.

1725
02:21:17,999 --> 02:21:20,126
Let's go get ice cream, kids.

1726
02:21:20,210 --> 02:21:21,544
But Marion bought ice cream.

1727
02:21:21,628 --> 02:21:22,795
I know a better place.

1728
02:21:22,879 --> 02:21:25,089
You can never have too much ice cream, right?

1729
02:21:28,968 --> 02:21:29,802
See you later.

1730
02:21:33,014 --> 02:21:36,392
A British sailor
is a soaring soul...

1731
02:21:36,476 --> 02:21:39,395
...as free as a mountain bird.

1732
02:21:39,479 --> 02:21:41,397
His mighty fist...

1733
02:21:41,481 --> 02:21:43,900
...must be ready to withstand...

1734
02:21:45,777 --> 02:21:47,070
That looks bad.

1735
02:21:48,571 --> 02:21:49,822
Does it hurt here?

1736
02:21:52,367 --> 02:21:53,826
Everything hurts.

1737
02:21:56,704 --> 02:21:58,331
I know what that feels like.

1738
02:22:04,420 --> 02:22:07,090
Where doesn't it hurt?

1739
02:22:15,723 --> 02:22:16,558
Here.

1740
02:22:17,433 --> 02:22:18,685
It doesn't hurt here.

1741
02:22:26,943 --> 02:22:28,486
And here.

1742
02:22:52,093 --> 02:22:54,554
Jabari, Alia, slow down!

1743
02:22:54,637 --> 02:22:56,848
Helena! Hurry up!

1744
02:22:56,931 --> 02:22:59,684
I scream, you scream...

1745
02:22:59,767 --> 02:23:01,519
Slow down.

1746
02:23:01,603 --> 02:23:04,147
- I scream, you scream...
- I scream, I scream...

1747
02:23:04,230 --> 02:23:06,024
We all scream for ice cream!

1748
02:23:06,107 --> 02:23:07,275
Slow down.
